24
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:08,400
♪ and other science facts
la la la ♪

25
00:01:08,433 --> 00:01:11,133
♪ then repeat to yourself
it’s just a show ♪

26
00:01:11,167 --> 00:01:14,167
♪ I should really
just relax ♪

27
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:17,333
♪ for mystery science theater ♪

28
00:01:17,367 --> 00:01:19,967
♪ 3000 ♪♪

29
00:01:22,433 --> 00:01:25,667
They promised me students,
but all I got were monkeys.

30
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:27,633
Monkeys!
Monkeys!

31
00:01:27,667 --> 00:01:29,733
So I took off my wetsuit,
dropped that hedge clipper,

32
00:01:29,767 --> 00:01:31,733
and walked out of the zoo
forever!

33
00:01:31,767 --> 00:01:33,467
Well, you’ve created
quite a little world

34
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:35,967
for yourself, Larry.

35
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Hey, tell me about
when you went mad now.

36
00:01:38,033 --> 00:01:40,133
Well, it was the ice capades,

37
00:01:40,167 --> 00:01:41,967
and I was pop-riveting
my kneecaps

38
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:43,333
to Peggy fleming’s
zamboni.

39
00:01:43,367 --> 00:01:47,000
Or maybe it was ’56,
sun valley.

40
00:01:47,033 --> 00:01:49,400
I was found behind
the soft-serve machine

41
00:01:49,433 --> 00:01:51,967
drooling over a picture
of dick button.

42
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,733
Or perhaps Oslo, when I was
found drunk and woozy,

43
00:01:55,767 --> 00:01:57,433
scratching the name
"toller Cranston"

44
00:01:57,467 --> 00:01:59,100
into my upper thigh
with a nail.

45
00:01:59,133 --> 00:02:01,133
You see, when i--
- is that when you went mad?

46
00:02:01,167 --> 00:02:03,033
- No, that’s when I became
a scientist.

47
00:02:03,067 --> 00:02:04,600
Oh... oh!

48
00:02:04,633 --> 00:02:06,700
Speaking of science,
it’s time to call Joel.

49
00:02:06,733 --> 00:02:08,067
We have to start
the experiment.

50
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:09,167
The movie’s great
this week.

51
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:11,067
Oh, yes, yes.
Come in, Joel,

52
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:13,200
you fancy-pantsy
Nancy boy.

53
00:02:13,233 --> 00:02:16,367
Hey, sirs, i’m ready for
this week’s invention exchange.

54
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:18,300
Bring it in a little bit,
cambot.

55
00:02:18,333 --> 00:02:20,333
This is a special purse
I developed

56
00:02:20,367 --> 00:02:22,300
for women who are
sick and tired

57
00:02:22,333 --> 00:02:25,033
of the constant threat
of getting their purse snatched.

58
00:02:25,067 --> 00:02:27,500
See how it looks like
an ordinary handbag.

59
00:02:27,533 --> 00:02:32,567
When street thugs open it,
voila!

60
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,633
Hell in a handbag.

61
00:02:35,667 --> 00:02:38,667
Nice use of pain,
joelmeister.

62
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:40,400
Now here’s ours.

63
00:02:40,433 --> 00:02:43,100
As you know,
toy manufacturers are always

64
00:02:43,133 --> 00:02:45,000
making their toys
too safe.

65
00:02:45,033 --> 00:02:46,667
And what children want
is realism.

66
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:48,200
And danger!

67
00:02:48,233 --> 00:02:50,067
That’s why we created
the acetylene-powered

68
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:51,533
thunder lizard.

69
00:02:51,567 --> 00:02:54,133
Yes, it’s a role-playing game,
and it comes complete

70
00:02:54,167 --> 00:02:56,067
with these costume glasses.

71
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:57,567
Just set up
your field of play,

72
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:01,100
then reach out
and torch someone.

73
00:03:01,133 --> 00:03:03,433
[Laughing]
- I like it!

74
00:03:03,467 --> 00:03:05,467
Look at him go!
Heh heh heh.

75
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:07,367
Oh, that’s gotta hurt.

76
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:11,067
Wow, that’s really sick
and twisted.

77
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:13,000
[Both]
Thank you.

78
00:03:13,033 --> 00:03:15,200
Now here’s something that’ll
really rub you wrong.

79
00:03:15,233 --> 00:03:17,133
Yes, it’s one
of our favorites.

80
00:03:17,167 --> 00:03:19,500
It’s called "mad monster."

81
00:03:19,533 --> 00:03:21,300
Oh, it’s got a neat
little laboratory

82
00:03:21,333 --> 00:03:22,667
tucked behind a bookcase,

83
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:24,667
and a couch you can
strap victims to!

84
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:28,533
Yes, and the screen is alive
with crepe hair and spirit gum.

85
00:03:28,567 --> 00:03:30,600
Oh, oh, and we have
the second installment

86
00:03:30,633 --> 00:03:32,300
of "rocket men on the moon."
Oh... ecch!

87
00:03:32,333 --> 00:03:35,700
Yes, this one is called
"the molten terror."

88
00:03:35,733 --> 00:03:38,600
[Larry laughing
fiendishly]

89
00:03:38,633 --> 00:03:42,233
[Alarm buzzer blaring]
Oh, movie sign!

90
00:03:42,267 --> 00:03:45,267
[Industrial clanks
and thunks]

91
00:03:50,433 --> 00:03:56,200
♪♪

92
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:01,067
I know, I gotta get wheels
for you, sweet Tom.

93
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:03,967
Those scientists are
really dorks.

94
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,867
It’s pumpkin boy again.

95
00:04:10,967 --> 00:04:15,500
"Radar men from the moon."

96
00:04:15,533 --> 00:04:18,300
Hey, it stars the same people
as last week.

97
00:04:18,333 --> 00:04:21,067
Aw, it’s just "featuring"
the same people as last week.

98
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:23,133
Featuring, sorry.

99
00:04:23,167 --> 00:04:26,500
These are the extras.
They’ll probably get killed.

100
00:04:26,533 --> 00:04:29,367
Dale Van sickel never made
a bad film.

101
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:31,633
He never made a real film.

102
00:04:31,667 --> 00:04:35,000
♪♪

103
00:04:42,167 --> 00:04:45,367
Consolidated film industries.

104
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:49,000
Franklin ad--
oh.

105
00:04:49,033 --> 00:04:51,167
We can only dream.

106
00:04:54,133 --> 00:04:58,300
Chapter two:
"Molten terror"!

107
00:04:58,333 --> 00:05:02,300
Ooh, isn’t terror bad enough
without being molten?

108
00:05:02,333 --> 00:05:05,200
"Commando Cody fights
the moon men in an effort

109
00:05:05,233 --> 00:05:07,833
"to escape retik,
who plans to disintegrate him

110
00:05:07,867 --> 00:05:10,300
with a ray pistol."

111
00:05:10,333 --> 00:05:12,700
Oh, then they’re gonna
work things out.

112
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:21,200
Hey, Joel, didn’t we
see this last time?

113
00:05:21,233 --> 00:05:23,833
Yeah, they’re just
recapping what happened.

114
00:05:23,867 --> 00:05:26,267
Well, he blew up the same.

115
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:29,167
But he continued to fight
with the sock man.

116
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:31,600
Willard Scott then reloaded
his cheese pistol.

117
00:05:36,067 --> 00:05:38,200
Isn’t that Marty feldman?

118
00:05:46,267 --> 00:05:49,033
Guns by vidal sassoon.

119
00:05:49,067 --> 00:05:52,700
This is where
we came in, you guys.

120
00:05:52,733 --> 00:05:53,700
Oh, if they would have
shown him

121
00:05:53,733 --> 00:05:55,133
diving out of the way
last time,

122
00:05:55,167 --> 00:05:57,467
I wouldn’t have spent the week
worrying about him.

123
00:06:00,767 --> 00:06:03,167
Nice punch.

124
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:06,667
He walked right into it.

125
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:10,167
Ugh.

126
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:12,400
Hmm, lobby, first floor.

127
00:06:12,433 --> 00:06:15,600
Tobacconist,
newspapers.

128
00:06:15,633 --> 00:06:19,633
I’m outside.

129
00:06:19,667 --> 00:06:21,633
Let’s see,
I stand in this corner,

130
00:06:21,667 --> 00:06:25,067
little tweak of the nipples,
and i’m outta here.

131
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:27,167
Oh-- oh, no!
Oh...

132
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:30,833
- Oh...
- Amazing.

133
00:06:30,867 --> 00:06:33,333
You’ll believe
a man can fly.

134
00:06:33,367 --> 00:06:35,700
Meanwhile,
back at mesa verde.

135
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:41,067
That’s gotta be hard
on the arches,

136
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:42,233
I don’t care where
you’re from.

137
00:06:42,267 --> 00:06:44,100
Oh, I think those fell
a long time ago.

138
00:06:44,133 --> 00:06:45,133
I’ll be in my trailer.

139
00:06:45,167 --> 00:06:47,233
Hope there’s some
deli food left.

140
00:06:47,267 --> 00:06:53,100
And no brown m&m’s
this time.

141
00:06:53,133 --> 00:06:55,100
[Sputtering hiss]
Oh, how rude!

142
00:06:55,133 --> 00:06:56,833
So retik plans
to invade the earth

143
00:06:56,867 --> 00:06:58,333
with the zombies
from the moon

144
00:06:58,367 --> 00:07:00,433
as soon as the saboteurs
he already has there

145
00:07:00,467 --> 00:07:02,267
finish softening up
our defenses.

146
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:03,433
Do you think he’ll be able
to do it?

147
00:07:03,467 --> 00:07:05,067
I’m afraid he might.

148
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:07,167
I don’t know how many men
or rocket ships he has,

149
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:09,200
but his atomic weapons
are so far ahead

150
00:07:09,233 --> 00:07:11,700
of anything we have, he wouldn’t
need a very big army.

151
00:07:11,733 --> 00:07:13,600
What did you find out
about the ray guns?

152
00:07:13,633 --> 00:07:15,000
Ron and Nancy?

153
00:07:15,033 --> 00:07:17,200
They use an element called
lumarium instead of uranium

154
00:07:17,233 --> 00:07:19,033
as the base for their
atomic reactions.

155
00:07:19,067 --> 00:07:20,600
And they take an hour
to load.

156
00:07:20,633 --> 00:07:21,733
They currently can control
the reactions

157
00:07:21,767 --> 00:07:23,500
so they will work either
as a disintegrating ray

158
00:07:23,533 --> 00:07:26,000
or as an explosion,
like our atomic bomb.

159
00:07:26,033 --> 00:07:27,667
Well, if their weapons are
that powerful,

160
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:30,100
it would be impossible for our
people to attack them here.

161
00:07:30,133 --> 00:07:32,333
Stop whining.

162
00:07:32,367 --> 00:07:34,400
She’s got a real hairy nape.

163
00:07:34,433 --> 00:07:36,167
Apparently,
they let us land because

164
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:37,800
they weren’t afraid
of a single ship,

165
00:07:37,833 --> 00:07:40,200
but they would undoubtedly
blast it at an invading fleet.

166
00:07:40,233 --> 00:07:42,233
Then hadn’t we better
go back right away

167
00:07:42,267 --> 00:07:43,400
and warn our people?

168
00:07:43,433 --> 00:07:45,200
Our orders were to find out
if the attacks

169
00:07:45,233 --> 00:07:46,500
were coming from here.

170
00:07:46,533 --> 00:07:47,800
And if so, to try
to figure out

171
00:07:47,833 --> 00:07:49,067
some way to stop them.

172
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:50,633
Those are large orders.
All we have is

173
00:07:50,667 --> 00:07:52,500
a light machine gun
and some hand grenades.

174
00:07:52,533 --> 00:07:54,133
They wouldn’t do much
to that walled city.

175
00:07:54,167 --> 00:07:57,067
No, but there was one
of they ray guns

176
00:07:57,100 --> 00:07:58,467
in retik’s laboratory.

177
00:07:58,500 --> 00:07:59,967
If I could get hold
of that,

178
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:01,433
it would be easy
to blast open

179
00:08:01,467 --> 00:08:03,667
their pressurized buildings and
really put them out of business.

180
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:05,500
Then we’ll have a sale.

181
00:08:05,533 --> 00:08:07,433
That may not be too tough.

182
00:08:07,467 --> 00:08:09,567
We brought some nitrous oxide
with us, didn’t we?

183
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:11,233
Yes, there’s a small tank
of it.

184
00:08:11,267 --> 00:08:12,733
Why did we bring that,
anyway?

185
00:08:12,767 --> 00:08:13,833
Who’s gonna be first.

186
00:08:13,867 --> 00:08:15,567
They got it from Dennis hopper,
I think.

187
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:18,833
I’ll find the air intake that
leads to retik’s laboratory

188
00:08:18,867 --> 00:08:20,233
and turn the gas
into it.

189
00:08:20,267 --> 00:08:22,200
That ought to put them
to sleep long enough

190
00:08:22,233 --> 00:08:23,633
to let me get away
with the ray gun.

191
00:08:23,667 --> 00:08:25,133
If it’s like the gun
you found on earth,

192
00:08:25,167 --> 00:08:27,367
it’ll be too heavy to carry,
let alone fly with.

193
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:29,667
That’s right, but the tripod
isn’t important.

194
00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:31,633
I’ll dismantle the gun
from the tripod.

195
00:08:31,667 --> 00:08:33,233
I think I can handle
the gun all right.

196
00:08:33,267 --> 00:08:35,200
- He’s feeling pretty good.
- It’ll be quite a load.

197
00:08:35,233 --> 00:08:37,100
I better get a helmet
and go with you.

198
00:08:37,133 --> 00:08:39,200
Hank and Joan can stay here
and guard the ship.

199
00:08:39,233 --> 00:08:42,067
Okay. I may need some help
at that.

200
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:43,533
Dibs on the rocket pack!

201
00:08:43,567 --> 00:08:46,333
I got it-- no, my turn.
Oh, shoot.

202
00:08:49,467 --> 00:08:51,533
Oh, so they’re both
walking now.

203
00:08:51,567 --> 00:08:53,567
They’re "walking on the moon."

204
00:09:01,400 --> 00:09:03,033
You better not come
any closer.

205
00:09:03,067 --> 00:09:05,167
I’ll fly on in
and get the gun.

206
00:09:05,200 --> 00:09:07,233
I’ll manage to lug it back
this far myself.

207
00:09:07,267 --> 00:09:08,533
With the whole town
chasing you?

208
00:09:08,567 --> 00:09:10,067
Good thing they move
so we know who’s talking.

209
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:11,633
I don’t think they can get
around out side very well.

210
00:09:11,667 --> 00:09:13,167
How come they got
Groucho Marx moustaches

211
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:15,100
on their helmets?

212
00:09:18,367 --> 00:09:20,533
Nipple tweak,
and i’m off.

213
00:09:20,567 --> 00:09:21,567
This is getting easy.

214
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:23,067
I love it
when he does that.

215
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:25,433
That’s so cool.

216
00:09:25,467 --> 00:09:28,233
He’s gonna fly right into
the commercial, I think.

217
00:09:32,233 --> 00:09:34,600
Hey, Diana Ross is playing
at caesar’s palace.

218
00:09:34,633 --> 00:09:36,733
And speaking
of Diana Ross...

219
00:09:50,200 --> 00:09:53,500
That looks just like the roof
of my old grade school.

220
00:09:53,533 --> 00:09:56,067
Let’s see, just hook up
that nitrous oxide

221
00:09:56,100 --> 00:09:57,767
to the air-intake shaft,

222
00:09:57,800 --> 00:10:00,567
then we can do some serious
dental work in there.

223
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:02,433
What’s he gonna do, anyway,
take out their fillings

224
00:10:02,467 --> 00:10:04,467
and serve them
really cold drinks?

225
00:10:04,500 --> 00:10:06,500
Maybe it’s really helium,

226
00:10:06,533 --> 00:10:08,067
and they’ll sound
like the munchkins.

227
00:10:08,100 --> 00:10:09,467
Let’s see, what’s
a four-letter word

228
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:11,067
that means "lunar"?

229
00:10:11,100 --> 00:10:12,533
Planet?
No, too many letters.

230
00:10:12,567 --> 00:10:16,233
[Gas hissing]

231
00:10:16,267 --> 00:10:19,033
There he goes!

232
00:10:19,067 --> 00:10:23,500
Woozy. No,
that doesn’t fit either.

233
00:10:23,533 --> 00:10:26,300
Hookah... hookah.

234
00:10:26,333 --> 00:10:28,167
Something’s wrong
with the ventilating system.

235
00:10:28,200 --> 00:10:32,167
- Get the oxygen.
- No, it’s my nap time.

236
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:34,733
It’s not that hard
of a job.

237
00:10:38,067 --> 00:10:42,767
Man, they’re really
overacting in this thing.

238
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:44,433
Nice swoon.

239
00:10:44,467 --> 00:10:46,167
It’s not that heavy.
[Door creaks]

240
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:47,733
Oh, you’d think in the future
on the moon

241
00:10:47,767 --> 00:10:49,200
they’d at least
get rid of the creaks.

242
00:10:49,233 --> 00:10:50,733
Yeah.

243
00:10:58,300 --> 00:11:01,667
Faker, faker!
You’re dead.

244
00:11:11,100 --> 00:11:14,600
Hey, either of you guys got
a 9/16 wrench?

245
00:11:14,633 --> 00:11:17,733
I’ll just use
this church key here.

246
00:11:17,767 --> 00:11:20,067
Smell my fingers.

247
00:11:20,100 --> 00:11:22,100
You’re breathing
for two now.

248
00:11:24,267 --> 00:11:28,067
- Buddy breathing.
- That’s better, huh?

249
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:42,300
Oh, great, hit him in the face
and hurt your hand.

250
00:11:42,333 --> 00:11:44,267
Dumb-o.

251
00:11:52,367 --> 00:11:54,633
Just give him
a headbutt.

252
00:11:54,667 --> 00:11:57,000
Give him a headbutt,
he’s just wearing spandex

253
00:11:57,033 --> 00:12:01,100
on his head.

254
00:12:01,133 --> 00:12:03,767
That’s for Bryant gumble!

255
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:06,600
That’s for gene shalit.

256
00:12:06,633 --> 00:12:09,133
And that’s for dressing up
like Carmen Miranda!

257
00:12:09,167 --> 00:12:11,267
God, that was dumb!

258
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:14,333
Those are all Bob mackie
creations, aren’t they?

259
00:12:17,067 --> 00:12:18,533
Someone’s gonna snag
their jammies

260
00:12:18,567 --> 00:12:21,067
on something sharp,
I can tell.

261
00:12:23,733 --> 00:12:27,067
Earth chair!
Earth chair!

262
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:28,533
Harelip!
Harelip!

263
00:12:28,567 --> 00:12:30,733
See if they bust a vase.

264
00:12:35,467 --> 00:12:38,633
I’ve never seen so many
lava lamps in my life.

265
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:47,533
Hope he remembers
what level he parked on.

266
00:12:47,567 --> 00:12:49,800
I’m in the moon man lot.

267
00:12:53,133 --> 00:12:56,267
Maybe they won’t notice me
carrying this.

268
00:12:56,300 --> 00:12:59,100
It I act really
nonchalant...

269
00:12:59,133 --> 00:13:00,867
Guards!
Guards!

270
00:13:00,900 --> 00:13:02,433
The earthman has escaped
with the ray gun.

271
00:13:02,467 --> 00:13:04,233
Send a car out
to search for him!

272
00:13:04,267 --> 00:13:06,300
And don’t forget
the cheese.

273
00:13:10,133 --> 00:13:11,633
It’s a moon car.

274
00:13:11,667 --> 00:13:15,100
And it’s filled
with evil michelin men.

275
00:13:15,133 --> 00:13:19,167
What a goofy-looking
vehicle.

276
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:20,467
They better hurry.

277
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:24,733
They gotta get that car back
to the amusement park.

278
00:13:24,767 --> 00:13:26,700
It’s a e-ticket ride,
you know.

279
00:13:26,733 --> 00:13:28,533
It sure is heavy.

280
00:13:28,567 --> 00:13:30,567
- Is anybody after you?
- Not yet.

281
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:32,767
Give me a hand with this.
We’ve got to move fast.

282
00:13:32,800 --> 00:13:36,467
Is it just me, or do those guys
look like the crash dummies?

283
00:13:36,500 --> 00:13:38,233
"You could learn a lot
from a dummy.

284
00:13:38,267 --> 00:13:39,233
Buckle up."

285
00:13:45,200 --> 00:13:46,567
He must have come
this way.

286
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:47,667
He couldn’t have got by
the guards

287
00:13:47,700 --> 00:13:49,233
at the canyon bridge.

288
00:13:49,267 --> 00:13:52,200
No, and he won’t move
very fast carrying that gun.

289
00:13:56,167 --> 00:13:59,167
Those fins really
keep it stable.

290
00:13:59,200 --> 00:14:01,067
I didn’t know they had
plywood on the moon.

291
00:14:01,100 --> 00:14:03,133
There they are.
Give them a blast.

292
00:14:03,167 --> 00:14:07,200
Hey, that thing’s
really gonna come in handy

293
00:14:07,233 --> 00:14:08,633
when Alan Shepard
gets to the moon.

294
00:14:08,667 --> 00:14:10,033
It’s gonna make
a swell golf cart.

295
00:14:10,067 --> 00:14:11,467
Let’s try to keep going.

296
00:14:11,500 --> 00:14:13,400
Maybe we can dodge around
those rocks and lose ’em.

297
00:14:13,433 --> 00:14:15,733
Maybe you could use the gun.

298
00:14:15,767 --> 00:14:18,200
You know, the big one
that blows up things...

299
00:14:18,233 --> 00:14:20,633
Like moon cars, perhaps?

300
00:14:30,767 --> 00:14:34,267
I wonder if they know
how stupid they look right now.

301
00:14:41,333 --> 00:14:43,333
They’re still after us.
Let’s go.

302
00:14:43,367 --> 00:14:45,167
Give him the hershey’s
head kiss of death.

303
00:14:48,700 --> 00:14:52,800
Kind of looks like
an electric razor.

304
00:14:52,833 --> 00:14:57,267
Even our name says
merry Christmas.

305
00:14:57,300 --> 00:14:59,100
That looks like a cave.
Let’s get in there.

306
00:14:59,133 --> 00:15:01,433
Good.

307
00:15:01,467 --> 00:15:05,533
It’s a moon mirage...
Dolt.

308
00:15:18,167 --> 00:15:20,233
It died.

309
00:15:20,267 --> 00:15:22,133
Those old two-cycles,
you know.

310
00:15:22,167 --> 00:15:24,567
We can’t follow them
in there.

311
00:15:24,600 --> 00:15:26,633
Set the ray gun
at constant heat.

312
00:15:26,667 --> 00:15:28,867
Oh, constance heat!
I love her films!

313
00:15:28,900 --> 00:15:31,567
[Ray gun activating loudly]

314
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:33,600
You know,
that constant-heat ray

315
00:15:33,633 --> 00:15:36,633
sounds a lot like
a Chevy horn.

316
00:15:39,700 --> 00:15:43,100
This must be the molten terror
of the title.

317
00:15:43,133 --> 00:15:47,133
They’re turning that mountain
into Taffy.

318
00:15:47,167 --> 00:15:50,300
I think they may turn
our heroes into Taffy soon.

319
00:15:50,333 --> 00:15:53,467
It’s a big rock candy
mountain.

320
00:15:53,500 --> 00:15:56,267
Snot canyon!

321
00:15:56,300 --> 00:15:59,267
[Ray gun blaring]

322
00:16:03,567 --> 00:16:04,533
What’s that?

323
00:16:04,567 --> 00:16:07,167
It’s the land of dairy queen,

324
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:09,900
and a river of chocolate’s
coming right for you.

325
00:16:09,933 --> 00:16:12,800
- Never mind the gun!
- Isn’t that lava?

326
00:16:12,833 --> 00:16:14,500
Well, i’ll guess
they’ll be cooked,

327
00:16:14,533 --> 00:16:15,667
but their hands
will be clean.

328
00:16:15,700 --> 00:16:17,833
Pumice action.

329
00:16:21,267 --> 00:16:23,533
What would you do now,
crow?

330
00:16:23,567 --> 00:16:25,733
- I’d, uh, panic.
- Panic?

331
00:16:25,767 --> 00:16:28,667
Tom?
- Oh, same, no question.

332
00:16:28,700 --> 00:16:30,400
There’s gotta be a way out.
Here it comes!

333
00:16:30,433 --> 00:16:31,733
Whoa.

334
00:16:31,767 --> 00:16:33,633
This must be what it’s like
to be in the stomach

335
00:16:33,667 --> 00:16:37,333
of someone drinking
a malt.

336
00:16:37,367 --> 00:16:39,633
- What do you think?
- Look out.

337
00:16:47,200 --> 00:16:48,767
Blow on it.

338
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:51,200
I think we gotta
get out of here, you guys.

339
00:16:51,233 --> 00:16:56,100
- What a sticky mess.
- It’s almost time to go.

340
00:16:56,133 --> 00:16:57,833
You coming?

341
00:16:57,867 --> 00:17:00,600
Looks like the floor
of a movie theater.

342
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:18,200
[Industrial clanks
and thunks]

343
00:17:23,167 --> 00:17:25,733
♪ Macho macho bot ♪

344
00:17:25,767 --> 00:17:28,200
♪ i’ve got to be
a macho-- ♪

345
00:17:28,233 --> 00:17:31,400
Hey, excuse me, miss.
Hey.

346
00:17:31,433 --> 00:17:33,733
I’ve never noticed you
on the ship before.

347
00:17:33,767 --> 00:17:36,700
I’m Tom Servo,
man about satellite.

348
00:17:36,733 --> 00:17:38,867
You know, I know
I look small and all,

349
00:17:38,900 --> 00:17:40,500
but i’m really built
like a cuisinart.

350
00:17:40,533 --> 00:17:41,833
At least,
that’s what they tell me.

351
00:17:41,867 --> 00:17:44,233
You know, you’re
kinda quiet.

352
00:17:44,267 --> 00:17:45,633
I like that in a woman,
really.

353
00:17:45,667 --> 00:17:47,200
You know, too many
of the gals I know

354
00:17:47,233 --> 00:17:49,067
just like to rub
exotic oils on me

355
00:17:49,100 --> 00:17:50,167
and fan me, you know.

356
00:17:50,200 --> 00:17:51,633
It’s okay,
if you know what I mean.

357
00:17:51,667 --> 00:17:53,633
Well, maybe you don’t.
I don’t know.

358
00:17:53,667 --> 00:17:54,667
I need a change, though.

359
00:17:54,700 --> 00:17:56,200
I need a woman
more my speed,

360
00:17:56,233 --> 00:17:57,933
and I noticed you have
11 of ’em.

361
00:17:57,967 --> 00:18:01,200
Oh, did I offend you.
I’m sorry.

362
00:18:01,233 --> 00:18:02,767
I see you’re blushing.

363
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:04,433
No, that’s tomato juice.

364
00:18:04,467 --> 00:18:06,233
I love a woman
with juice in their head.

365
00:18:06,267 --> 00:18:07,333
It’s really a turn-on
to me,

366
00:18:07,367 --> 00:18:09,233
my little scientific
calculator.

367
00:18:09,267 --> 00:18:11,167
[Chuckles]
Ever gotten a wild hair

368
00:18:11,200 --> 00:18:12,800
and just filled your head
with guacamole

369
00:18:12,833 --> 00:18:14,300
just for the hell of it?

370
00:18:14,333 --> 00:18:16,400
I’m the kind of robot who likes
to throw caution to the wind,

371
00:18:16,433 --> 00:18:17,433
if you know what I mean.

372
00:18:17,467 --> 00:18:19,200
And if you do,
please tell me.

373
00:18:19,233 --> 00:18:21,200
[Laughs]

374
00:18:21,233 --> 00:18:24,233
Hey, I see you’ve still got
a power cord.

375
00:18:24,267 --> 00:18:26,200
I like a woman with
a healthy power source.

376
00:18:26,233 --> 00:18:27,700
I really do.

377
00:18:27,733 --> 00:18:29,800
You know, i’m coming on
a bit strong, I know,

378
00:18:29,833 --> 00:18:31,867
and i’m sorry,
but I love your lines.

379
00:18:31,900 --> 00:18:33,733
God, you’ve got
classic features.

380
00:18:33,767 --> 00:18:36,133
Crush, grate, chop, whip.

381
00:18:36,167 --> 00:18:37,567
Baby, you’ve got it all!

382
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:39,467
[Blender whirring]

383
00:18:39,500 --> 00:18:42,200
Wha-- oh, and a lovely
singing voice, too.

384
00:18:42,233 --> 00:18:44,867
Oh, you do have it all.
Joel, i’m in lov--

385
00:18:44,900 --> 00:18:47,133
Joel? Joel!
Joel--

386
00:18:47,167 --> 00:18:49,233
that’s it,
the gauntlet’s been thrown!

387
00:18:49,267 --> 00:18:50,733
Nobody drinks from my gal!
Let’s go, come on!

388
00:18:50,767 --> 00:18:53,200
What are you talking about, Tom?

389
00:18:53,233 --> 00:18:56,233
My gal... i’m in love,
and you’re drinking from her!

390
00:18:56,267 --> 00:18:59,433
- Let’s go!
- Tom... Tom, it’s a blender.

391
00:18:59,467 --> 00:19:02,167
- Wha--
- it’s a blender.

392
00:19:02,200 --> 00:19:04,233
- Oh, blender.
I knew that.

393
00:19:04,267 --> 00:19:07,467
♪ Macho macho bot
i’ve got to be ♪

394
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:09,133
Excuse me, miss,
i’ve noticed you--

395
00:19:09,167 --> 00:19:13,133
oh, excuse me,
Mr. Coffee.

396
00:19:20,233 --> 00:19:24,367
♪♪

397
00:19:24,400 --> 00:19:26,667
- All right...
- Okay...

398
00:19:26,700 --> 00:19:29,900
There you go.
[Sighs]

399
00:19:29,933 --> 00:19:32,467
Okay.

400
00:19:32,500 --> 00:19:34,333
Gotta look at ’em
on an angle.

401
00:19:34,367 --> 00:19:36,367
Uh, nope.
This is it over here.

402
00:19:36,400 --> 00:19:38,233
- That’s better.
- They’re so hard to read.

403
00:19:38,267 --> 00:19:41,100
Left, right.
Hmm.

404
00:19:41,133 --> 00:19:42,333
They should have gotten
the guy out of the light

405
00:19:42,367 --> 00:19:43,333
before they shot this.

406
00:19:43,367 --> 00:19:47,133
- Yeah.
- It’s the cameraman.

407
00:19:47,167 --> 00:19:50,100
You should be at more
of an angle, crow.

408
00:19:50,133 --> 00:19:52,167
- Switch.
- Oh, that helps.

409
00:19:52,200 --> 00:19:55,233
No, it’s the same.

410
00:19:55,267 --> 00:19:57,467
Now straight.
Okay.

411
00:19:57,500 --> 00:20:00,867
[Howling]
Kitty!

412
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:03,700
They must have some sort
of leash law there.

413
00:20:03,733 --> 00:20:07,400
Oh, they do.
Pretty extreme.

414
00:20:07,433 --> 00:20:10,133
Oh, he’s got a Thorn
in his paw.

415
00:20:10,167 --> 00:20:12,200
I don’t think that’s a Thorn.

416
00:20:12,233 --> 00:20:15,500
I think it’s an I.V.
Yeah.

417
00:20:15,533 --> 00:20:17,200
Oh, he’s giving plasma.

418
00:20:17,233 --> 00:20:19,167
Must be down on his luck.
Poor dog.

419
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:21,700
Acme coyote transfuser.

420
00:20:27,333 --> 00:20:29,633
Now for the thorough
examination.

421
00:20:29,667 --> 00:20:32,233
[Chuckles]
Just relax.

422
00:20:32,267 --> 00:20:34,200
Hey, it’s bill Bixby
as Lou ferrigno.

423
00:20:34,233 --> 00:20:38,700
[Howling]

424
00:20:38,733 --> 00:20:41,800
Yes, I know, you’d like
to join your brothers outside

425
00:20:41,833 --> 00:20:43,800
and howl at the moon.

426
00:20:43,833 --> 00:20:46,433
But you’re serving
a much better purpose.

427
00:20:46,467 --> 00:20:49,233
Yes, you’re serving science
through me.

428
00:20:49,267 --> 00:20:50,267
[Howling]

429
00:20:50,300 --> 00:20:52,733
And he’s making
union scale.

430
00:20:52,767 --> 00:20:56,267
Oh, he’ll soon have him
housebroken.

431
00:20:56,300 --> 00:20:58,933
And it will be painful.

432
00:20:58,967 --> 00:21:01,167
I could have had a v8.

433
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:04,267
You know, he’s very protective
about the secret recipe

434
00:21:04,300 --> 00:21:06,267
of his sun tea.

435
00:21:06,300 --> 00:21:09,800
It’s a bit of the hair
of the dog--

436
00:21:09,833 --> 00:21:11,200
how does that go, Joel?

437
00:21:11,233 --> 00:21:12,233
Hair of the dog
that bit you.

438
00:21:12,267 --> 00:21:13,533
Yeah.

439
00:21:13,567 --> 00:21:15,800
Let’s see,
three parts gin,

440
00:21:15,833 --> 00:21:20,367
one part vermouth.

441
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:22,667
I like mine very dry.

442
00:21:22,700 --> 00:21:27,467
Hair designs
by shemp Howard.

443
00:21:27,500 --> 00:21:29,767
He’s got an itch.

444
00:21:29,800 --> 00:21:31,333
[Groans]

445
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:40,133
Are you quite comfortable,
Petro?

446
00:21:40,167 --> 00:21:41,467
Sure, doctor.

447
00:21:41,500 --> 00:21:43,167
Just because his circulation’s
cut off

448
00:21:43,200 --> 00:21:44,733
at his chest, hands,
and waist...

449
00:21:44,767 --> 00:21:46,233
I have no reason
to be afraid.

450
00:21:46,267 --> 00:21:48,833
That’s right, Petro, you have
nothing to be afraid of.

451
00:21:48,867 --> 00:21:51,133
Nothing at all.

452
00:21:51,167 --> 00:21:52,200
[Wolf howling]

453
00:21:52,233 --> 00:21:53,833
This is gonna hurt the dog
a lot more

454
00:21:53,867 --> 00:21:55,600
than it’s gonna hurt me.

455
00:21:55,633 --> 00:21:58,600
[Howling]

456
00:21:58,633 --> 00:22:00,867
There, did that hurt?

457
00:22:00,900 --> 00:22:04,700
No, that was just
like a pin sticking me.

458
00:22:07,700 --> 00:22:11,733
How come I have to have
these straps on, doc?

459
00:22:11,767 --> 00:22:15,233
Well, you see, Petro,
i’m trying a new formula.

460
00:22:15,267 --> 00:22:17,367
I wouldn’t want you to make
a sudden move

461
00:22:17,400 --> 00:22:19,200
and fall off the couch.

462
00:22:19,233 --> 00:22:22,133
But don’t worry, it’ll only be
for a little while.

463
00:22:28,900 --> 00:22:31,867
How do you feel now?

464
00:22:31,900 --> 00:22:34,533
I feel just like
I always did.

465
00:22:34,567 --> 00:22:36,400
When you stick me
with that thing...

466
00:22:36,433 --> 00:22:41,767
- Kinda dopey.
- ... i’m sleepy.

467
00:22:41,800 --> 00:22:45,900
♪ Rock-a-bye monster
on the little bed ♪

468
00:22:45,933 --> 00:22:50,167
♪ when you wake up
some townsfolk will be dead ♪

469
00:22:50,200 --> 00:22:54,200
That’s right...
Go to sleep.

470
00:23:09,733 --> 00:23:11,767
He’s changing.

471
00:23:11,800 --> 00:23:15,300
Looks kinda like
Fred travalena now.

472
00:23:15,333 --> 00:23:18,467
Kind of--
he’s growing hair.

473
00:23:18,500 --> 00:23:22,133
My god, he’s turned him
into Abe Lincoln!

474
00:23:22,167 --> 00:23:24,433
The rail splitter.

475
00:23:24,467 --> 00:23:29,667
Wow.

476
00:23:29,700 --> 00:23:31,533
Isaac Asimov!

477
00:23:31,567 --> 00:23:34,567
Lecherous scientist.

478
00:23:34,600 --> 00:23:37,233
Amish farmer.

479
00:23:37,267 --> 00:23:39,567
Quaker oats man.

480
00:23:47,600 --> 00:23:50,567
Dr. Cornelius
from "planet of the apes."

481
00:23:58,667 --> 00:24:01,300
A cure for baldness
at last.

482
00:24:01,333 --> 00:24:03,867
I’m calling it
Helsinki formula.

483
00:24:03,900 --> 00:24:05,633
- Line.
- Line!

484
00:24:05,667 --> 00:24:08,233
Line, please.

485
00:24:08,267 --> 00:24:11,367
Gentlemen, I wish you were here
to see the proof of my claim,

486
00:24:11,400 --> 00:24:13,667
that the transfusion of blood
between different species

487
00:24:13,700 --> 00:24:16,033
is possible.

488
00:24:16,067 --> 00:24:19,233
A few moments ago,
Petro was a man.

489
00:24:19,267 --> 00:24:21,733
A harmless,
good-natured man.

490
00:24:21,767 --> 00:24:24,367
- Now bingo is his name-o.
- Look at him now.

491
00:24:24,400 --> 00:24:26,300
He looks just like one
of the flying monkeys

492
00:24:26,333 --> 00:24:29,167
from "the wizard of oz."

493
00:24:33,233 --> 00:24:36,267
Now he’s his own
best friend.

494
00:24:36,300 --> 00:24:38,233
[Growling, muttering]

495
00:24:38,267 --> 00:24:40,267
[Growling]

496
00:24:40,300 --> 00:24:43,500
He’s no longer human.
He’s a wolf.

497
00:24:43,533 --> 00:24:45,567
Snarling, ferocious.

498
00:24:45,600 --> 00:24:47,333
Lusting for the kill.

499
00:24:47,367 --> 00:24:48,833
He needs to go outside,
too, I think.

500
00:24:48,867 --> 00:24:54,400
This is a scientific
miracle, gentlemen.

501
00:24:54,433 --> 00:24:56,300
Hey, it’s William s. Burroughs.

502
00:24:56,333 --> 00:24:57,700
You’re a madman, Cameron.

503
00:24:57,733 --> 00:24:59,533
Your claim is
ridiculous.

504
00:24:59,567 --> 00:25:02,400
That’s exactly what you said
in the newspapers,

505
00:25:02,433 --> 00:25:06,233
professor Blaine--
that I was a madman.

506
00:25:06,267 --> 00:25:08,267
I may be mad,
but you’re transparent.

507
00:25:08,300 --> 00:25:11,233
... science chair
at the university.

508
00:25:11,267 --> 00:25:14,667
Perhaps you’ll change
your mind one day soon,

509
00:25:14,700 --> 00:25:17,767
when Petro tears
at your throat.

510
00:25:17,800 --> 00:25:19,967
Poppycock!

511
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:21,467
But look.

512
00:25:21,500 --> 00:25:23,900
[Petro snarling]

513
00:25:23,933 --> 00:25:26,400
I don’t think they should
let him get on the furniture.

514
00:25:26,433 --> 00:25:28,667
He’s getting all
tangled up.

515
00:25:28,700 --> 00:25:31,667
Even now, he’d like to be
at his work.

516
00:25:31,700 --> 00:25:33,633
- Ripping out throats.
- My catalytic agent

517
00:25:33,667 --> 00:25:36,367
has brought about
a complete transition

518
00:25:36,400 --> 00:25:39,300
from man to wolf.

519
00:25:39,333 --> 00:25:42,300
Your crazy experiments are
a disgrace to science.

520
00:25:42,333 --> 00:25:44,300
- Mr. Mooney.
- Gail Gordon.

521
00:25:44,333 --> 00:25:46,633
Those are the very words
you used in denouncing me

522
00:25:46,667 --> 00:25:48,233
to the faculty.

523
00:25:48,267 --> 00:25:52,400
Professor Fitzgerald...
Science!

524
00:25:52,433 --> 00:25:54,800
What do you know
about science?

525
00:25:54,833 --> 00:25:58,167
You with your hidebound
dogmas and shriveled mind

526
00:25:58,200 --> 00:25:59,633
that refused to recognize
progress...

527
00:25:59,667 --> 00:26:01,700
Hey, it’s col. Potter.
Harry Morgan.

528
00:26:01,733 --> 00:26:02,900
What is progress to you
with your foolish

529
00:26:02,933 --> 00:26:04,333
tampering with nature?

530
00:26:04,367 --> 00:26:06,667
I’m glad you asked
that question,

531
00:26:06,700 --> 00:26:09,267
professor Hatfield.

532
00:26:09,300 --> 00:26:11,333
You also were one
of those stupid fools

533
00:26:11,367 --> 00:26:12,867
who raised their voices
against me.

534
00:26:12,900 --> 00:26:15,800
So he’s talking to us.

535
00:26:15,833 --> 00:26:17,400
I’ve got a question.

536
00:26:17,433 --> 00:26:19,667
But our armed forces
are not in combat

537
00:26:19,700 --> 00:26:22,267
with a savage horde
who fight with fanatical fury!

538
00:26:22,300 --> 00:26:23,500
Don’t think he heard you.

539
00:26:23,533 --> 00:26:25,833
Well, that fanatical fury
will avail them nothing

540
00:26:25,867 --> 00:26:27,833
when I place my new serum
at the disposal

541
00:26:27,867 --> 00:26:29,500
of the war department.

542
00:26:29,533 --> 00:26:33,233
Just picture, gentlemen,
an army of wolfmen,

543
00:26:33,267 --> 00:26:36,567
fearless, raging...
- Shedding.

544
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:38,400
Every man a snarling animal.

545
00:26:38,433 --> 00:26:39,800
And an army of people
behind ’em

546
00:26:39,833 --> 00:26:40,900
with pooper scoopers.

547
00:26:40,933 --> 00:26:42,933
... millions of such
animal men.

548
00:26:42,967 --> 00:26:45,567
Men who are governed
by one collective thought--

549
00:26:45,600 --> 00:26:48,633
the animal lust to kill without
regard of personal safety.

550
00:26:48,667 --> 00:26:49,933
[All]
Republicans?

551
00:26:49,967 --> 00:26:51,967
Such an army will be
invincible, gentlemen.

552
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:55,800
Such an army will sweep
everything before it.

553
00:26:55,833 --> 00:26:59,567
Your scheme is too
utterly fantastic, Dr. Cameron.

554
00:26:59,600 --> 00:27:02,400
You’re fools, all of you.
Four blind fools.

555
00:27:02,433 --> 00:27:03,833
Especially you,
Dr. Warwick.

556
00:27:03,867 --> 00:27:05,933
♪ Four blind fools ♪

557
00:27:05,967 --> 00:27:08,667
How would you control
these wolfmen?

558
00:27:08,700 --> 00:27:10,767
I’ve perfected
an antidote

559
00:27:10,800 --> 00:27:13,867
that induces the return
of immediate normalcy.

560
00:27:13,900 --> 00:27:17,333
And I suppose that it would be
an easy matter

561
00:27:17,367 --> 00:27:19,300
to round up a million
wild animals

562
00:27:19,333 --> 00:27:21,700
and administer an antidote.

563
00:27:21,733 --> 00:27:25,633
No, Cameron, you’re mad.
Stark raving mad.

564
00:27:25,667 --> 00:27:28,400
- And I for one am in favor--
- silence!

565
00:27:28,433 --> 00:27:29,700
I have a whistle.

566
00:27:29,733 --> 00:27:32,900
I’m not interested
in your imbecilic mouthings.

567
00:27:32,933 --> 00:27:35,300
All of you have demonstrated
your lack of vision

568
00:27:35,333 --> 00:27:37,733
by demanding my resignation
from the faculty.

569
00:27:37,767 --> 00:27:39,200
And your lack of bodies.

570
00:27:39,233 --> 00:27:40,800
Well, you accomplished
your purpose.

571
00:27:40,833 --> 00:27:42,433
You cast me out.

572
00:27:42,467 --> 00:27:46,167
You robbed me of everything
that I held most dear in life--

573
00:27:46,200 --> 00:27:50,300
- position, honor, respect.
- My own parking spot.

574
00:27:50,333 --> 00:27:51,933
You branded me
as a madman.

575
00:27:51,967 --> 00:27:54,900
Held me up to ridicule
before the whole world.

576
00:27:54,933 --> 00:27:57,533
But now it’s time
for my revenge.

577
00:27:57,567 --> 00:28:01,733
- Petro will see to that.
- Sorry we said anything.

578
00:28:01,767 --> 00:28:03,800
There’s no escape
for any of you.

579
00:28:03,833 --> 00:28:05,867
You shall die
one by one

580
00:28:05,900 --> 00:28:08,300
at the hands
of he scientific marvel

581
00:28:08,333 --> 00:28:09,667
that you scoffed at.

582
00:28:09,700 --> 00:28:11,233
What a grouch.

583
00:28:11,267 --> 00:28:12,933
You can’t intimidate us
with fantastic threats.

584
00:28:12,967 --> 00:28:15,333
You’re a faker, Cameron,
a mad faker!

585
00:28:15,367 --> 00:28:16,600
We’ll have you ostracized

586
00:28:16,633 --> 00:28:18,400
by every scientist
in the country.

587
00:28:18,433 --> 00:28:20,200
There are institutions
for madmen.

588
00:28:20,233 --> 00:28:21,467
Like the Pentagon.

589
00:28:21,500 --> 00:28:23,933
And we’ll see that you’re
confined in one.

590
00:28:23,967 --> 00:28:26,200
You would think, since
it was his imagination,

591
00:28:26,233 --> 00:28:28,400
he’d at least have them
be afraid of them.

592
00:28:28,433 --> 00:28:30,967
They left.
He should have served drinks,

593
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:35,267
canapes, those little sandwiches
with the crust cut off.

594
00:28:35,300 --> 00:28:37,933
- Nut cups.
- You will see, gentlemen.

595
00:28:37,967 --> 00:28:39,433
You will see.

596
00:28:39,467 --> 00:28:40,700
[Petro growling]

597
00:28:40,733 --> 00:28:44,667
Next comes the ghost
of Christmas present.

598
00:28:44,700 --> 00:28:46,333
Christmas present,
for me?

599
00:28:46,367 --> 00:28:49,833
Okay, boy, okay,
i’ll get you a liver snap.

600
00:28:49,867 --> 00:28:54,367
[Petro snarling]

601
00:28:54,400 --> 00:28:57,600
I hope he puts it
on his nose.

602
00:28:57,633 --> 00:28:59,267
[Wolf howling]

603
00:28:59,300 --> 00:29:01,767
What’s the matter, boy?

604
00:29:01,800 --> 00:29:03,433
Skippy.

605
00:29:03,467 --> 00:29:06,733
♪♪

606
00:29:06,767 --> 00:29:09,167
I’d say skippy is what
he was eating.

607
00:29:09,200 --> 00:29:12,233
♪♪

608
00:29:12,267 --> 00:29:16,767
Look, I will revolutionize
shaving forever.

609
00:29:16,800 --> 00:29:20,400
Hey, he looks okay.

610
00:29:20,433 --> 00:29:23,767
Or as okay
as he ever will.

611
00:29:23,800 --> 00:29:26,233
I bet his breath is
terrible, though.

612
00:29:26,267 --> 00:29:29,433
But his mouth is cleaner
than yours and mine.

613
00:29:41,800 --> 00:29:46,367
Tell me, doctor, how long
have I been asleep?

614
00:29:46,400 --> 00:29:48,200
Oh, about an hour,
I should say.

615
00:29:48,233 --> 00:29:49,300
That’s seven hours
to you and me.

616
00:29:49,333 --> 00:29:51,400
- How do you feel?
- I feel dog tired.

617
00:29:51,433 --> 00:29:52,867
I have a terrible headache.

618
00:29:52,900 --> 00:29:56,367
And I had some kind
of an awful dream.

619
00:29:56,400 --> 00:29:59,367
I was running all over
the countryside,

620
00:29:59,400 --> 00:30:02,567
chasing people
and trying to kill ’em.

621
00:30:02,600 --> 00:30:07,200
What does a dream
like that mean, doctor?

622
00:30:07,233 --> 00:30:08,367
Oh, that doesn’t mean anything.

623
00:30:08,400 --> 00:30:10,200
You just had a nightmare,
that’s all.

624
00:30:10,233 --> 00:30:13,200
But why should I be trying
to kill people?

625
00:30:13,233 --> 00:30:14,900
Even in my dreams,

626
00:30:14,933 --> 00:30:17,600
I ain’t got nothin’
against nobody.

627
00:30:17,633 --> 00:30:19,600
Well, let’s not talk
about it anymore.

628
00:30:19,633 --> 00:30:21,867
Go to bed.
Our work’s done for the night.

629
00:30:21,900 --> 00:30:24,300
I set up some newspapers
in the corner for you.

630
00:30:24,333 --> 00:30:28,833
All right, doc.

631
00:30:28,867 --> 00:30:32,700
Duh, tell me about
the rabbits again, George.

632
00:30:51,300 --> 00:30:53,700
And these are doors,
Petro.

633
00:30:56,200 --> 00:30:58,633
- Evening, miss lenora.
- Good evening, Petro.

634
00:30:58,667 --> 00:31:00,400
Well, night owl,
I was just coming down

635
00:31:00,433 --> 00:31:02,233
to find out how much longer
you’re going to work.

636
00:31:02,267 --> 00:31:03,800
Do you realize
how late it is?

637
00:31:03,833 --> 00:31:06,300
No, I didn’t, I was
so absorbed in my work.

638
00:31:06,333 --> 00:31:08,700
We done a lot
of work tonight.

639
00:31:08,733 --> 00:31:12,700
And I slept almost an hour,
and I had the awfulest dream--

640
00:31:12,733 --> 00:31:15,233
go to bed, Petro!

641
00:31:15,267 --> 00:31:18,367
Yes, doctor.
Good night, miss lenora.

642
00:31:18,400 --> 00:31:21,567
- Good night, Petro.
- I’ll be lurking for you.

643
00:31:21,600 --> 00:31:23,800
Dad, what kind of experiments
are you doing

644
00:31:23,833 --> 00:31:25,367
that you need Petro?

645
00:31:25,400 --> 00:31:28,233
Oh, he just helps me
in the laboratory

646
00:31:28,267 --> 00:31:29,367
moving the heavy things around.

647
00:31:29,400 --> 00:31:30,800
He’s a very strong man
is Petro.

648
00:31:30,833 --> 00:31:33,000
How much longer are we
gonna be here, dad?

649
00:31:33,033 --> 00:31:34,800
Why?

650
00:31:34,833 --> 00:31:36,133
’cause the place
gets on my nerves.

651
00:31:36,167 --> 00:31:37,500
I hate it.

652
00:31:37,533 --> 00:31:39,733
Your hating it has nothing
to do with being separated

653
00:31:39,767 --> 00:31:42,633
from that young reporter friend
of yours, has it?

654
00:31:42,667 --> 00:31:44,167
It has.
And I want to know

655
00:31:44,200 --> 00:31:45,533
when we’re going back
to the city.

656
00:31:45,567 --> 00:31:47,833
Very soon now, dear.
My work’s practically finished.

657
00:31:47,867 --> 00:31:49,333
[Petro howling]

658
00:31:49,367 --> 00:31:50,800
What was that?

659
00:31:50,833 --> 00:31:52,433
It was Petro,
talking in his sleep again.

660
00:31:52,467 --> 00:31:54,300
- What?
- Well, that noise.

661
00:31:54,333 --> 00:31:55,967
It sounded like
a wild animal howling.

662
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:57,900
I heard the same thing
last night.

663
00:31:57,933 --> 00:32:01,267
Oh, that’s probably
some dog in the neighborhood.

664
00:32:01,300 --> 00:32:03,267
Dogs do howl, you know.

665
00:32:03,300 --> 00:32:06,433
You mustn’t let things
like that upset you.

666
00:32:06,467 --> 00:32:08,333
Now, come along, dear...

667
00:32:08,367 --> 00:32:11,400
She want to be Judy garland
in the worst way, doesn’t she?

668
00:32:11,433 --> 00:32:15,233
I think so.

669
00:32:15,267 --> 00:32:18,233
Hey, it looks just like the
front of the munsters’ house.

670
00:32:18,267 --> 00:32:20,600
Without the charm.

671
00:32:26,700 --> 00:32:28,167
Good morning, Petro.

672
00:32:28,200 --> 00:32:30,167
Good mornin’, miss lenora.

673
00:32:30,200 --> 00:32:33,233
I was hopin’ you’d come
to the garden this mornin’.

674
00:32:33,267 --> 00:32:35,267
I got somethin’ for ya.

675
00:32:35,300 --> 00:32:36,667
It’s a human head!

676
00:32:36,700 --> 00:32:38,867
Oh, they’re lovely.
Thank you.

677
00:32:38,900 --> 00:32:41,867
I’ll pick them for you
every day if you like ’em.

678
00:32:41,900 --> 00:32:44,233
Well, that’s very kind
of you.

679
00:32:44,267 --> 00:32:46,800
I want you to meet my brother,
Fred gwynne.

680
00:32:46,833 --> 00:32:48,767
What’s the matter,
miss lenora?

681
00:32:48,800 --> 00:32:51,267
Petro, I don’t like
this place.

682
00:32:51,300 --> 00:32:53,733
No, I guess you feel
about this place

683
00:32:53,767 --> 00:32:55,267
the same as I do.

684
00:32:55,300 --> 00:32:56,633
I’m a’feared of it.

685
00:32:56,667 --> 00:32:58,867
Especially at night,
when the mist comes up

686
00:32:58,900 --> 00:33:00,500
out of the swamp.

687
00:33:00,533 --> 00:33:02,633
Of course, it’s all right
when the sun’s shinin’

688
00:33:02,667 --> 00:33:04,700
like it is this mornin’.

689
00:33:04,733 --> 00:33:06,667
Are there any wild animals
around here?

690
00:33:06,700 --> 00:33:09,467
- Just you, fur ball.
- There’s lots of ’em.

691
00:33:09,500 --> 00:33:13,367
- How come you ask?
- I was just curious.

692
00:33:13,400 --> 00:33:16,733
Say, Petro, what do you do
when you work with dad

693
00:33:16,767 --> 00:33:17,900
in the laboratory?

694
00:33:17,933 --> 00:33:20,433
Oh, I sit, I roll over,
and I shake sometimes.

695
00:33:20,467 --> 00:33:22,467
I don’t rightly know.

696
00:33:22,500 --> 00:33:25,033
Besides, I ain’t supposed
to tell none of it.

697
00:33:25,067 --> 00:33:27,067
Your pop made me promise.

698
00:33:27,100 --> 00:33:30,400
Well, you can trust me.
I wouldn’t tell anybody.

699
00:33:30,433 --> 00:33:32,467
Well, we--

700
00:33:32,500 --> 00:33:34,067
cheese it, it’s Alan Brady!

701
00:33:34,100 --> 00:33:37,633
No, I ain’t sayin’ nothin’.

702
00:33:37,667 --> 00:33:40,500
Petro, did you fetch
the paper this morning?

703
00:33:40,533 --> 00:33:41,433
- Good morning, dad.
- Did you sleep well?

704
00:33:41,467 --> 00:33:42,867
Yes, very well.

705
00:33:42,900 --> 00:33:44,800
Look at the flowers
that Petro gathered for me.

706
00:33:44,833 --> 00:33:46,700
Very pretty.

707
00:33:46,733 --> 00:33:48,267
I’m afraid you’ll have
to give up your gardening

708
00:33:48,300 --> 00:33:49,700
for the time being, Petro.

709
00:33:49,733 --> 00:33:51,267
- Aww.
- I want you in the laboratory.

710
00:33:51,300 --> 00:33:53,400
Well, doc, if I don’t keep
at the stuff,

711
00:33:53,433 --> 00:33:55,600
how am I ever gonna
make it look pretty?

712
00:33:55,633 --> 00:33:57,267
And I like pretty things.

713
00:33:57,300 --> 00:33:59,000
We shall leave this place
long before you’re able

714
00:33:59,033 --> 00:34:00,500
to beautify it.

715
00:34:00,533 --> 00:34:02,800
The gardening really isn’t
very important.

716
00:34:02,833 --> 00:34:04,233
Come along with me.

717
00:34:04,267 --> 00:34:07,367
Gardening always takes
a backseat to science.

718
00:34:07,400 --> 00:34:09,967
Always.

719
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:12,667
Ah, Petro, I see you’re
waiting for me.

720
00:34:12,700 --> 00:34:15,433
[Wolf howling]

721
00:34:15,467 --> 00:34:18,667
Hey, it’s a dog bell.

722
00:34:18,700 --> 00:34:21,567
You mustn’t be so jumpy--
you’re as nervous as that wolf.

723
00:34:21,600 --> 00:34:22,700
He sure is...

724
00:34:22,733 --> 00:34:24,000
- Is this like
mice and men

725
00:34:24,033 --> 00:34:25,567
or flowers for algernon,
Joel?

726
00:34:25,600 --> 00:34:26,667
Neither--
just keep watching.

727
00:34:26,700 --> 00:34:28,033
Sorry.

728
00:34:28,067 --> 00:34:29,567
And he ate (indistinct).

729
00:34:29,600 --> 00:34:31,400
All right, lie down, Petro.

730
00:34:31,433 --> 00:34:34,667
Are you gonna strap me down
again tonight, doc?

731
00:34:34,700 --> 00:34:36,867
Well, certainly--
it’s more necessary tonight

732
00:34:36,900 --> 00:34:38,600
than ever before.

733
00:34:38,633 --> 00:34:42,433
Gee, it must be great
to be educated.

734
00:34:42,467 --> 00:34:45,000
I wish I had a lot
of book learnin’, so...

735
00:34:45,033 --> 00:34:47,967
So I could strap people
down, too.

736
00:34:48,000 --> 00:34:51,333
Fortunately, you don’t need
education or intelligence

737
00:34:51,367 --> 00:34:53,067
for your part
of the experiment.

738
00:34:53,100 --> 00:34:56,967
Just strength...
Animal strength.

739
00:34:57,000 --> 00:34:59,000
Now, lie down,
and we’ll get to work.

740
00:34:59,033 --> 00:35:00,667
Now cough.

741
00:35:04,600 --> 00:35:07,267
Seatbelts save lives.

742
00:35:08,667 --> 00:35:11,300
The captain has turned off
the "no smoking" signs.

743
00:35:11,333 --> 00:35:13,433
Feel free to roam
about the swamps.

744
00:35:13,467 --> 00:35:14,967
[Wolf howling]

745
00:35:26,500 --> 00:35:28,267
Oh, Petro?

746
00:35:28,300 --> 00:35:30,200
There’s a kitty
out in the yard.

747
00:35:34,733 --> 00:35:37,667
♪♪

