is utterly fantastic.

999
00:46:28,533 --> 00:46:29,500
Oh, you like it?

1000
00:46:29,533 --> 00:46:30,767
I’m sorry,
I didn’t know

1001
00:46:30,800 --> 00:46:33,433
I was getting into
such deep water.

1002
00:46:33,467 --> 00:46:35,600
Well, i’ll hang my head in shame
and slink away.

1003
00:46:35,633 --> 00:46:36,600
Now, no harm done.

1004
00:46:36,633 --> 00:46:37,933
Don’t take it so to heart.

1005
00:46:37,967 --> 00:46:39,700
Put your feet up on the desk
and stay for a while.

1006
00:46:39,733 --> 00:46:41,400
Oh, I can’t now, i’ve got
to get back to the office.

1007
00:46:41,433 --> 00:46:42,433
But i’ll drop around
again.

1008
00:46:42,467 --> 00:46:44,033
Thanks very much
for the information.

1009
00:46:44,067 --> 00:46:45,533
I didn’t give you very much.

1010
00:46:45,567 --> 00:46:47,900
Bye, dagwood!

1011
00:46:47,933 --> 00:46:48,900
[Door closes]

1012
00:46:48,933 --> 00:46:51,100
Hi, clock.
[Clock chimes]

1013
00:46:53,467 --> 00:46:56,467
Time flies when you can’t
write script parts.

1014
00:46:56,500 --> 00:46:58,867
- Or edit.
- You know, I never understand

1015
00:46:58,900 --> 00:47:00,633
these "fireside" comics.

1016
00:47:00,667 --> 00:47:02,700
There’s a caveman and a bear
and, uh...

1017
00:47:02,733 --> 00:47:04,933
[Knocking on door]
Oh!

1018
00:47:04,967 --> 00:47:06,367
[Knocking continues]

1019
00:47:06,400 --> 00:47:07,967
Who is it?

1020
00:47:13,467 --> 00:47:15,767
- Hello, friend.
- [Humming ominous music]

1021
00:47:15,800 --> 00:47:17,733
- Surprised to see me?
- Yes, I confess I am.

1022
00:47:17,767 --> 00:47:18,933
Come in, sit down.

1023
00:47:18,967 --> 00:47:20,800
Thank you.

1024
00:47:20,833 --> 00:47:22,933
You shouldn’t
be surprised, Blaine.

1025
00:47:22,967 --> 00:47:26,367
I told you that i’d come back
when I could prove my theory

1026
00:47:26,400 --> 00:47:27,733
to your
complete satisfaction.

1027
00:47:27,767 --> 00:47:30,400
You say you’ve proven
that wild theory?

1028
00:47:30,433 --> 00:47:31,800
Well, i’m here.

1029
00:47:31,833 --> 00:47:34,200
You don’t think I enjoyed
your comments so much

1030
00:47:34,233 --> 00:47:36,500
that I came back merely
to hear you repeat them?

1031
00:47:36,533 --> 00:47:38,567
Joel, what did he do wrong,
anyway?

1032
00:47:38,600 --> 00:47:40,067
Oh, he was
the one out of five doctors

1033
00:47:40,100 --> 00:47:41,567
who didn’t choose bayer,
crow.

1034
00:47:41,600 --> 00:47:42,667
Oh.

1035
00:47:42,700 --> 00:47:44,167
... any statement proven
to be untrue.

1036
00:47:44,200 --> 00:47:46,800
Do you think that
will cancel your obligation?

1037
00:47:46,833 --> 00:47:48,667
Do you think a retraction
will pay me

1038
00:47:48,700 --> 00:47:52,400
for the humiliation of being
held up to public ridicule?

1039
00:47:52,433 --> 00:47:55,500
For having my scientific
reputation blasted?

1040
00:47:55,533 --> 00:47:59,333
For being forced to resign
from an honorable position?

1041
00:47:59,367 --> 00:48:00,933
Oh, chill out,
Mr. Salty.

1042
00:48:00,967 --> 00:48:03,300
I deeply regret
any possible injustice.

1043
00:48:03,333 --> 00:48:04,733
But my rejection
of your theory

1044
00:48:04,767 --> 00:48:08,033
was not intended
as a personal persecution.

1045
00:48:08,067 --> 00:48:09,900
I’m sorry, i--

1046
00:48:09,933 --> 00:48:12,067
it wasn’t a love note,
either.

1047
00:48:12,100 --> 00:48:14,000
I supposed we’re all prone
at times to lose sight

1048
00:48:14,033 --> 00:48:15,100
of the true perspective.

1049
00:48:15,133 --> 00:48:16,467
Well, let’s
say no more about it.

1050
00:48:16,500 --> 00:48:20,033
I’ll gladly say anything
in support of your theory.

1051
00:48:20,067 --> 00:48:22,467
I’m not asking you to take
my word for anything.

1052
00:48:22,500 --> 00:48:24,467
Just believe the evidence
of your own eyes.

1053
00:48:24,500 --> 00:48:25,633
Petro.

1054
00:48:30,133 --> 00:48:31,933
[Imitating lurch]
You rang?

1055
00:48:31,967 --> 00:48:33,600
Hey, you made the hat fit!

1056
00:48:39,500 --> 00:48:41,400
Petro, you sit here.

1057
00:48:43,133 --> 00:48:44,433
My gimmick.

1058
00:48:44,467 --> 00:48:46,700
So what?
You made a big dumb guy.

1059
00:48:49,567 --> 00:48:52,567
Sit on the cedar chips,
fella.

1060
00:48:52,600 --> 00:48:56,633
You have any shiny objects
for my friend over here?

1061
00:48:59,800 --> 00:49:02,533
You are aware
of the nature of this?

1062
00:49:02,567 --> 00:49:04,833
We’ve been doing this
a long time.

1063
00:49:04,867 --> 00:49:06,567
You may rest assure,
the experiment

1064
00:49:06,600 --> 00:49:08,133
is a proven success.

1065
00:49:08,167 --> 00:49:10,700
Otherwise,
I wouldn’t be here.

1066
00:49:10,733 --> 00:49:13,433
What good can
come with tampering

1067
00:49:13,467 --> 00:49:15,533
with the normal laws
of nature?

1068
00:49:15,567 --> 00:49:17,133
- Teflon?
- Uh, microwave milkshakes?

1069
00:49:17,167 --> 00:49:19,500
Room air fresheners.

1070
00:49:19,533 --> 00:49:21,567
You’ll understand
when you see the results.

1071
00:49:21,600 --> 00:49:25,567
I abhor whatever appears to be
a perversion of science.

1072
00:49:25,600 --> 00:49:27,900
Mingling the blood
of man and beast

1073
00:49:27,933 --> 00:49:28,967
is downright sacrilege.

1074
00:49:29,000 --> 00:49:30,400
Tell that to the NFL.

1075
00:49:30,433 --> 00:49:33,100
I have no sympathy with such
maudlin sentimentality.

1076
00:49:33,133 --> 00:49:35,500
I propounded a theory
which you denounced publicly

1077
00:49:35,533 --> 00:49:37,333
as being ridiculous,

1078
00:49:37,367 --> 00:49:41,400
the result
of a disordered mind.

1079
00:49:41,433 --> 00:49:44,467
Are you trying to squirm
out of facing

1080
00:49:44,500 --> 00:49:46,733
the possibility
that you may be wrong?

1081
00:49:46,767 --> 00:49:47,733
Not at all.

1082
00:49:47,767 --> 00:49:49,733
I will cooperate
in any way possible.

1083
00:49:49,767 --> 00:49:51,500
Then you take
the injection.

1084
00:49:51,533 --> 00:49:52,967
Just what I wanted to hear.

1085
00:49:53,000 --> 00:49:54,333
I’ve given him
one injection

1086
00:49:54,367 --> 00:49:56,433
to prepare his blood
for the transfusion.

1087
00:49:56,467 --> 00:49:59,833
He’ll need another...
In exactly 20 minutes.

1088
00:49:59,867 --> 00:50:03,533
He’s hearing sounds
that we can’t hear.

1089
00:50:03,567 --> 00:50:06,900
He’s getting a whiff
of his own overalls.

1090
00:50:06,933 --> 00:50:09,600
They must be pretty gamey
by now.

1091
00:50:09,633 --> 00:50:13,133
[Phone ringing]

1092
00:50:13,167 --> 00:50:14,400
[Lifts receiver]

1093
00:50:14,433 --> 00:50:15,533
Hello?

1094
00:50:15,567 --> 00:50:18,833
Is this magnolia 3-7136?

1095
00:50:18,867 --> 00:50:21,967
I’d like to speak
to Dr. Lorenzo Cameron, please.

1096
00:50:22,000 --> 00:50:23,800
Uh, just a moment, please.

1097
00:50:23,833 --> 00:50:25,067
It’s for you, Cameron.

1098
00:50:25,100 --> 00:50:27,400
They want to know if we have
prince Albert in a can.

1099
00:50:27,433 --> 00:50:28,900
Let me take it--
I love that bit.

1100
00:50:28,933 --> 00:50:30,000
Oh, is that you, dear?

1101
00:50:30,033 --> 00:50:31,333
You asked me to call you

1102
00:50:31,367 --> 00:50:32,767
and find out if you’re
returning tonight.

1103
00:50:32,800 --> 00:50:34,933
It’s all right, i’ll be home
as soon as I can.

1104
00:50:34,967 --> 00:50:36,433
Good-bye.

1105
00:50:36,467 --> 00:50:38,067
Good-bye.

1106
00:50:39,500 --> 00:50:40,467
That dad of mine.

1107
00:50:40,500 --> 00:50:41,733
God, he’s nutty.

1108
00:50:41,767 --> 00:50:44,600
... friends, i’ll have
to go and get her.

1109
00:50:44,633 --> 00:50:45,600
I shan’t be long.

1110
00:50:45,633 --> 00:50:46,700
What about the second
injection?

1111
00:50:46,733 --> 00:50:49,433
You said it had
to be given in 20 minutes.

1112
00:50:49,467 --> 00:50:51,833
Oh, yes, the injection.

1113
00:50:51,867 --> 00:50:53,700
I may be able
to make it.

1114
00:50:53,733 --> 00:50:56,433
If not, I wonder, doctor,
whether you’d do it for me.

1115
00:50:56,467 --> 00:51:00,300
Why, uh...
Yes, I guess so.

1116
00:51:00,333 --> 00:51:02,133
That’s very, very kind
of you.

1117
00:51:02,167 --> 00:51:04,100
Petro, you stay here and don’t
bother Dr. Blaine.

1118
00:51:04,133 --> 00:51:06,100
No, sir,
I won’t bother him.

1119
00:51:06,133 --> 00:51:07,600
Of course, it’s quite
possible, doctor,

1120
00:51:07,633 --> 00:51:09,367
if I don’t get back
that you could ensure

1121
00:51:09,400 --> 00:51:12,333
the failure
of this demonstration

1122
00:51:12,367 --> 00:51:15,100
by forgetting
his injection.

1123
00:51:15,133 --> 00:51:16,500
I’ll have
to take that chance.

1124
00:51:16,533 --> 00:51:17,800
Don’t worry.

1125
00:51:17,833 --> 00:51:19,433
Even if I lacked
a sense of honor,

1126
00:51:19,467 --> 00:51:21,367
pride would hardly
allow me

1127
00:51:21,400 --> 00:51:23,367
to stoop to such a level.

1128
00:51:23,400 --> 00:51:26,633
I shall give him the injection
if you’re not here by 12:00.

1129
00:51:26,667 --> 00:51:30,967
At 12:00, precisely.

1130
00:51:31,000 --> 00:51:32,467
Oh, and if
he gets hungry,

1131
00:51:32,500 --> 00:51:34,867
there’s some jerky treats
in his knapsack.

1132
00:51:34,900 --> 00:51:36,633
Big jerky treats.

1133
00:51:39,833 --> 00:51:40,867
Who’s he calling?

1134
00:51:40,900 --> 00:51:42,400
The s.P.C.A.

1135
00:51:42,433 --> 00:51:43,967
What’s the s.P.C.A., crow?

1136
00:51:44,000 --> 00:51:47,533
Uh, society for the prevention
of the cruelty of actors.

1137
00:51:47,567 --> 00:51:49,167
- Oh.
- Heh heh heh.

1138
00:51:49,200 --> 00:51:50,433
Good one, Servo.

1139
00:51:50,467 --> 00:51:51,467
Nice setup.

1140
00:51:51,500 --> 00:51:53,967
- Hello?
- Ba-boom!

1141
00:51:54,000 --> 00:51:55,867
Yeah, is that you, Blaine?

1142
00:51:55,900 --> 00:51:57,567
- What you want to drag me...
- Just call him hemmingway.

1143
00:51:57,600 --> 00:51:59,000
... out of bed at this time
of the night, for?

1144
00:51:59,033 --> 00:52:01,767
I’ve just had
a surprising visitor.

1145
00:52:01,800 --> 00:52:03,500
Lorenzo Cameron.

1146
00:52:03,533 --> 00:52:04,867
Better than Lorenzo lamas.

1147
00:52:04,900 --> 00:52:07,100
He claims to have
proven his crazy,

1148
00:52:07,133 --> 00:52:09,667
outlandish blood transfusion
theory

1149
00:52:09,700 --> 00:52:11,967
and demands the right
to give a demonstration.

1150
00:52:12,000 --> 00:52:13,833
- Yay!
- Yeah.

1151
00:52:13,867 --> 00:52:15,033
Is Dave there?

1152
00:52:15,067 --> 00:52:16,000
No, he’s-- no.

1153
00:52:16,033 --> 00:52:17,567
No, he’s not here now.

1154
00:52:17,600 --> 00:52:18,933
When’s he
gonna be back?

1155
00:52:18,967 --> 00:52:21,133
He’ll be back after 12:00.

1156
00:52:21,167 --> 00:52:22,933
Why don’t you
come on over?

1157
00:52:22,967 --> 00:52:24,267
- Is there gonna be beer?
- All right.

1158
00:52:24,300 --> 00:52:26,567
I’ll get dressed
and be over right away.

1159
00:52:26,600 --> 00:52:28,900
Expect me in about
ten minutes.

1160
00:52:28,933 --> 00:52:30,567
Nice jammies.

1161
00:52:35,700 --> 00:52:38,100
Are you at all familiar
with the work

1162
00:52:38,133 --> 00:52:40,100
Dr. Cameron is doing?

1163
00:52:40,133 --> 00:52:41,433
Oh, no, sir.

1164
00:52:41,467 --> 00:52:43,933
I’m just handyman
around the place.

1165
00:52:43,967 --> 00:52:46,667
Nobody, not even
miss lenora,

1166
00:52:46,700 --> 00:52:48,467
knows what he does.

1167
00:52:48,500 --> 00:52:50,433
Oh, his daughter is still
with him?

1168
00:52:50,467 --> 00:52:52,500
Oh, yes, sir,
she’s with him.

1169
00:52:52,533 --> 00:52:54,667
But I think she’s lonesome.

1170
00:52:54,700 --> 00:52:57,600
She seems to be
grievin’ about somethin’.

1171
00:52:57,633 --> 00:53:00,000
Kind of like a big,
dumb Andy Griffith, isn’t he?

1172
00:53:00,033 --> 00:53:01,700
... no right to accept such
a sacrifice from her.

1173
00:53:01,733 --> 00:53:02,933
Well, a big Andy Griffith.

1174
00:53:02,967 --> 00:53:05,200
- Andy Griffith?
- I don’t like being busybody,

1175
00:53:05,233 --> 00:53:07,433
but she’s such a fine girl
and...

1176
00:53:07,467 --> 00:53:08,533
Just plain old
Andy Griffith.

1177
00:53:08,567 --> 00:53:09,767
Yeah.

1178
00:53:09,800 --> 00:53:12,000
... just the tonic she needs
to make her happy.

1179
00:53:12,033 --> 00:53:14,700
Where do they live?

1180
00:53:14,733 --> 00:53:18,567
Oh, Dr. Cameron rented the old
danfield homestead house,

1181
00:53:18,600 --> 00:53:22,433
about four or five
miles from Ashton.

1182
00:53:22,467 --> 00:53:24,000
It was a fine place,

1183
00:53:24,033 --> 00:53:27,433
but ain’t been nobody
lived there for a long time,

1184
00:53:27,467 --> 00:53:30,633
and the weeds kept growing
closer to the house

1185
00:53:30,667 --> 00:53:33,533
till they about
swallowed up everything.

1186
00:53:33,567 --> 00:53:34,567
Petro, give me a hand.

1187
00:53:34,600 --> 00:53:35,800
My thumbs are caught
in my vest.

1188
00:53:35,833 --> 00:53:38,967
Oh, that’s good.

1189
00:53:39,000 --> 00:53:42,500
I’m prescribing a weed eater
and some turf-builder, Petro.

1190
00:53:42,533 --> 00:53:43,500
Oh, and a lawnmower.

1191
00:53:43,533 --> 00:53:46,600
Take this
to a hardware store.

1192
00:53:46,633 --> 00:53:47,800
Blaine just told me that you
wanted to demonstrate

1193
00:53:47,833 --> 00:53:49,600
the proof of your
blood transfusion theory.

1194
00:53:49,633 --> 00:53:52,967
He exaggerated when
he mentioned demonstration.

1195
00:53:53,000 --> 00:53:55,700
I told him I could
prove my claim.

1196
00:53:55,733 --> 00:53:57,600
But I don’t think
professor Blaine

1197
00:53:57,633 --> 00:53:59,867
is in a cooperative
frame of mind.

1198
00:53:59,900 --> 00:54:02,667
- Cooperative.
- That’s why I came to you.

1199
00:54:02,700 --> 00:54:04,000
What do you
want me to do?

1200
00:54:04,033 --> 00:54:07,600
I’d like you to be present
when I talk to him.

1201
00:54:07,633 --> 00:54:09,933
All right, i’ll take
a walk over with you.

1202
00:54:11,500 --> 00:54:13,700
You know, uh...

1203
00:54:13,733 --> 00:54:17,500
Wood spikes, some of
those beefsteak tomatoes.

1204
00:54:17,533 --> 00:54:19,867
That last scene was blocked like
a bad high school play.

1205
00:54:19,900 --> 00:54:22,567
Hmm...

1206
00:54:22,600 --> 00:54:23,967
A pair of wellies,
then we can use your hat

1207
00:54:24,000 --> 00:54:25,533
as a composter.

1208
00:54:25,567 --> 00:54:28,900
[Clock chiming]

1209
00:54:31,100 --> 00:54:33,367
It’s injection time!

1210
00:54:37,500 --> 00:54:41,433
This should take a while.

1211
00:54:41,467 --> 00:54:44,400
12 bings and a sharp
pain in the arm.

1212
00:54:48,633 --> 00:54:50,467
Just shoot him.
You told him you would.

1213
00:54:50,500 --> 00:54:51,867
Don’t pad your part.

1214
00:54:55,133 --> 00:54:57,000
No, he’s back there.

1215
00:54:58,233 --> 00:54:59,600
Hey, turn around.

1216
00:54:59,633 --> 00:55:00,700
He’s not in the yard.

1217
00:55:00,733 --> 00:55:02,600
No.

1218
00:55:02,633 --> 00:55:06,067
No, it’s the big dumb guy
in the chair.

1219
00:55:06,100 --> 00:55:08,133
This is his big scene.

1220
00:55:12,167 --> 00:55:14,567
His last big shot before
all his hair is gone.

1221
00:55:17,133 --> 00:55:18,833
- Finally found him.
- I heartily disapprove

1222
00:55:18,867 --> 00:55:20,467
of the nature
of this experiment,

1223
00:55:20,500 --> 00:55:22,533
but my promise
to Dr. Cameron

1224
00:55:22,567 --> 00:55:24,467
leaves me no alternative.

1225
00:55:24,500 --> 00:55:25,567
Sure.

1226
00:55:25,600 --> 00:55:26,733
Roll up your sleeve.

1227
00:55:34,700 --> 00:55:36,767
It’s the original
"sid and Nancy."

1228
00:55:36,800 --> 00:55:37,933
How do you feel?

1229
00:55:37,967 --> 00:55:40,533
- Just like I always do.
- Just like I always do.

1230
00:55:40,567 --> 00:55:42,800
Kind of dopey.

1231
00:55:47,833 --> 00:55:49,067
Line.

1232
00:55:50,767 --> 00:55:52,967
He’s looking
right at his script.

1233
00:55:53,000 --> 00:55:55,167
- Line.
- Hey.

1234
00:56:02,033 --> 00:56:05,700
Well, his eyes
rolled back in his head.

1235
00:56:05,733 --> 00:56:08,167
Well, here we are.

1236
00:56:08,200 --> 00:56:10,133
Ohh.

1237
00:56:10,167 --> 00:56:11,900
- Something wrong?
- It’s his pen.

1238
00:56:11,933 --> 00:56:13,633
It just opened up
in his coat pocket.

1239
00:56:13,667 --> 00:56:15,467
Heart trouble.

1240
00:56:15,500 --> 00:56:17,767
Nothing serious, but...

1241
00:56:17,800 --> 00:56:19,833
Stressing, at times.

1242
00:56:19,867 --> 00:56:22,933
I’ll be all right
after I rest a bit.

1243
00:56:25,767 --> 00:56:29,633
Yes, I like
green eggs and ham.

1244
00:56:29,667 --> 00:56:34,800
Thank you, thank you,
Sam I am.

1245
00:56:37,800 --> 00:56:40,433
Maybe you better read him
"go, dog. Go!"

1246
00:56:43,167 --> 00:56:44,867
That’s it, Petro.

1247
00:56:44,900 --> 00:56:47,767
From here to there,
from there to here.

1248
00:56:47,800 --> 00:56:49,933
Maybe we’d better
go in the house.

1249
00:56:49,967 --> 00:56:52,533
Oh, no, it’s...
In-- in just a moment.

1250
00:56:55,233 --> 00:56:56,467
What’s the matter-- drunk?

1251
00:56:56,500 --> 00:56:57,500
Nope, scientist.

1252
00:56:57,533 --> 00:56:58,500
Want me to call
an ambulance?

1253
00:56:58,533 --> 00:56:59,600
No, I don’t want an ambulance.

1254
00:56:59,633 --> 00:57:01,900
I’ll be... all right
in a minute.

1255
00:57:01,933 --> 00:57:03,567
Well, I was
just trying to help.

1256
00:57:03,600 --> 00:57:06,500
♪♪

1257
00:57:10,033 --> 00:57:12,667
This Dr. Seuss, god,
he kills me.

1258
00:57:12,700 --> 00:57:13,700
[Chuckling]

1259
00:57:13,733 --> 00:57:16,667
♪♪

1260
00:57:16,700 --> 00:57:17,767
Wake up, boy.

1261
00:57:17,800 --> 00:57:19,100
[Whistling]

1262
00:57:19,133 --> 00:57:21,833
Wake up.

1263
00:57:21,867 --> 00:57:23,133
Whoa.

1264
00:57:23,167 --> 00:57:26,567
Hey, it’s wishbone
from the old "rawhide" show.

1265
00:57:26,600 --> 00:57:29,533
♪♪

1266
00:57:40,167 --> 00:57:42,533
He’s doing his cagney.

1267
00:57:42,567 --> 00:57:43,967
You dirty rat.

1268
00:57:44,000 --> 00:57:45,600
You’re the man
who murdered my brother.

1269
00:57:45,633 --> 00:57:47,900
You dirty rat.

1270
00:57:47,933 --> 00:57:52,233
I’m gonna rip your head off
like an all-day sucker.

1271
00:57:52,267 --> 00:57:54,100
[Monster snarling]

1272
00:57:54,133 --> 00:57:55,233
What’s that?

1273
00:57:55,267 --> 00:57:56,767
Sounded like it come
from the house!

1274
00:58:00,867 --> 00:58:02,533
Come on in here--
there’s something wrong.

1275
00:58:02,567 --> 00:58:04,800
Jim never drinks coffee
at home.

1276
00:58:10,667 --> 00:58:11,800
I’ll say there’s
something wrong.

1277
00:58:11,833 --> 00:58:13,633
No, i’ll say there’s
something wrong!

1278
00:58:13,667 --> 00:58:14,867
There’s something wrong.

1279
00:58:14,900 --> 00:58:16,800
Get me police headquarters,
and make it fast.

1280
00:58:20,500 --> 00:58:21,733
Hello, sarge,
this is dugan.

1281
00:58:21,767 --> 00:58:23,200
- I got a murder on my hands.
- Finally...

1282
00:58:23,233 --> 00:58:25,800
I get my harmonica back.

1283
00:58:25,833 --> 00:58:27,433
- It’s got spit all over it.
- Right away!

1284
00:58:31,633 --> 00:58:35,600
That’s the work of an animal,
not of a human being.

1285
00:58:35,633 --> 00:58:37,500
What do you
know about this?

1286
00:58:37,533 --> 00:58:39,567
How could I know anything?
I was with you.

1287
00:58:39,600 --> 00:58:40,967
Why so defensive then?

1288
00:58:41,000 --> 00:58:42,833
Officer, my heart’s beginning
to trouble me again.

1289
00:58:42,867 --> 00:58:44,200
I can’t be of any use here.

1290
00:58:44,233 --> 00:58:45,533
I may as well leave.

1291
00:58:45,567 --> 00:58:46,700
Sure, go ahead.

1292
00:58:46,733 --> 00:58:49,200
I can’t pretend
to feel any great sorrow.

1293
00:58:49,233 --> 00:58:51,000
But then, my feelings
are of no importance,

1294
00:58:51,033 --> 00:58:52,500
one way or the other.

1295
00:58:52,533 --> 00:58:54,567
Nobody loves me,
everybody hates me.

1296
00:58:54,600 --> 00:58:56,900
Think i’ll go eat worms.

1297
00:58:56,933 --> 00:58:59,967
What a kook.
Whew!

1298
00:59:00,000 --> 00:59:01,467
- Howdy, lieutenant.
- Hi.

1299
00:59:01,500 --> 00:59:04,200
I’ve seen
a lot of murders in my day,

1300
00:59:04,233 --> 00:59:07,467
but never as messy
as this one.

1301
00:59:07,500 --> 00:59:09,167
This was no
boating accident.

1302
00:59:09,200 --> 00:59:10,400
This was a shark.

1303
00:59:13,600 --> 00:59:16,600
This should make a gory enough
story for your paper.

1304
00:59:16,633 --> 00:59:18,433
This is more
than just a story to me.

1305
00:59:18,467 --> 00:59:19,533
He was my friend.

1306
00:59:19,567 --> 00:59:21,800
It’ll make a great story.

1307
00:59:21,833 --> 00:59:23,967
Maybe even a movie,
with a sequel.

1308
00:59:24,000 --> 00:59:25,600
... college professors
arguing outside

1309
00:59:25,633 --> 00:59:27,467
and using
a lot of big words.

1310
00:59:27,500 --> 00:59:28,733
They followed me in here.

1311
00:59:28,767 --> 00:59:30,433
And I heard one of them
say that

1312
00:59:30,467 --> 00:59:32,133
some kind of
a dangerous animal

1313
00:59:32,167 --> 00:59:33,467
done this killing.

1314
00:59:33,500 --> 00:59:34,667
Well, how could
a dangerous animal

1315
00:59:34,700 --> 00:59:36,033
be roaming around the city?

1316
00:59:36,067 --> 00:59:37,900
I don’t know--
must have got away from a zoo

1317
00:59:37,933 --> 00:59:39,433
or a circus or something.

1318
00:59:39,467 --> 00:59:42,700
What’s this-- "mulch,
turf-builder, leaf bags"?

1319
00:59:42,733 --> 00:59:43,900
Hmm.

1320
00:59:48,667 --> 00:59:52,133
Maybe i’ll just remove
this evidence from the scene.

1321
00:59:52,167 --> 00:59:53,800
- Ashton... Ashton...
- Ashton.

1322
00:59:53,833 --> 00:59:55,800
- You talking to me?
- We all fall down.

1323
00:59:55,833 --> 00:59:57,100
- No, no, i’m just trying
to remember something.

1324
00:59:57,133 --> 00:59:59,600
Ashton...
I got it.

1325
00:59:59,633 --> 01:00:01,733
An a.P. Story came in
from Ashton the other day

1326
01:00:01,767 --> 01:00:03,467
about a--
about a child being killed

1327
01:00:03,500 --> 01:00:05,500
by a weird animal
from the swampland.

1328
01:00:05,533 --> 01:00:06,767
What do you mean,
"weird animal"?

1329
01:00:06,800 --> 01:00:09,133
Platypus or a jackalope--
you know, weird.

1330
01:00:09,167 --> 01:00:10,633
... it traveled on two legs,
like a man.

1331
01:00:10,667 --> 01:00:12,167
Ah, he was crazy or drunk.

1332
01:00:12,200 --> 01:00:13,667
There ain’t
no such animal.

1333
01:00:13,700 --> 01:00:15,833
Anyway, how could he
get way up here?

1334
01:00:15,867 --> 01:00:18,733
I wish I knew.

1335
01:00:18,767 --> 01:00:21,900
Reel change...
Reel change!

1336
01:00:21,933 --> 01:00:23,133
Thank you.

1337
01:00:23,167 --> 01:00:24,567
[Wolf howling]

1338
01:00:24,600 --> 01:00:26,567
Tell me about it.

1339
01:00:26,600 --> 01:00:28,500
You think you got problems.

1340
01:00:28,533 --> 01:00:31,033
None of my clothes
fit anymore.

1341
01:00:31,067 --> 01:00:32,133
[Howling continues]

1342
01:00:33,200 --> 01:00:35,167
♪♪

1343
01:00:35,200 --> 01:00:37,133
And they’ll never be able
to get a serious role.

1344
01:00:41,467 --> 01:00:44,567
(Chuckling)

1345
01:00:44,600 --> 01:00:47,067
Do you think I look like
Victor mature?

1346
01:00:54,867 --> 01:00:55,800
She smells him first.

1347
01:00:55,833 --> 01:00:58,233
Petro,
you startled me!

1348
01:00:58,267 --> 01:00:59,467
Oh, what’s the matter?

1349
01:00:59,500 --> 01:01:00,833
Why do you stare at me
like that?

1350
01:01:00,867 --> 01:01:02,833
He’s wondering
what you’d look like on rye.

1351
01:01:02,867 --> 01:01:04,500
I don’t know,
miss lenora.

1352
01:01:04,533 --> 01:01:06,633
I feel funny.

1353
01:01:06,667 --> 01:01:09,800
I smell something in the mist,
coming up out of the swamp.

1354
01:01:09,833 --> 01:01:11,600
It’s like
a voice talking to me,

1355
01:01:11,633 --> 01:01:13,600
telling me to do
something.

1356
01:01:13,633 --> 01:01:14,567
Something terrible.

1357
01:01:14,600 --> 01:01:15,667
Oh, but that isn’t real.

1358
01:01:15,700 --> 01:01:17,500
That’s just
your imagination.

1359
01:01:17,533 --> 01:01:18,600
You mustn’t pay
any attention

1360
01:01:18,633 --> 01:01:20,667
to what that voice
seems to be saying.

1361
01:01:20,700 --> 01:01:22,700
Petro!

1362
01:01:22,733 --> 01:01:24,833
Bad dog!

1363
01:01:24,867 --> 01:01:27,100
You know you’re always
supposed to be in the kitchen.

1364
01:01:27,133 --> 01:01:29,567
Go to your room.

1365
01:01:29,600 --> 01:01:31,533
You’ve no right
in this part of the house.

1366
01:01:31,567 --> 01:01:32,533
Get out!

1367
01:01:32,567 --> 01:01:34,133
Immediately!

1368
01:01:34,167 --> 01:01:36,567
- Really bad dog.
- Wake up!

1369
01:01:36,600 --> 01:01:38,867
You’re dreaming.

1370
01:01:38,900 --> 01:01:41,500
Get a muzzle.

1371
01:01:41,533 --> 01:01:42,867
For the doctor.

1372
01:01:42,900 --> 01:01:45,633
I don’t know
what’s come over me.

1373
01:01:45,667 --> 01:01:48,600
I must have a touch
of swamp fever.

1374
01:01:48,633 --> 01:01:49,567
That’s very likely.
Now go to bed.

1375
01:01:49,600 --> 01:01:51,567
I’ll get something for you.

1376
01:01:51,600 --> 01:01:53,567
- Yes, sir.
- I had a marsh rash once.

1377
01:01:53,600 --> 01:01:57,133
I’m sorry if I made
a nuisance of myself.

1378
01:02:03,333 --> 01:02:04,733
Dad, what happened
to Petro?

1379
01:02:04,767 --> 01:02:06,533
I’m terribly afraid.

1380
01:02:06,567 --> 01:02:07,867
There’s nothing
to be afraid of, dear.

1381
01:02:07,900 --> 01:02:09,567
He just had a touch
of fever, that’s all.

1382
01:02:09,600 --> 01:02:10,767
That was more than fever.

1383
01:02:10,800 --> 01:02:12,900
Why, his eyes were
the eyes of a wild beast.

1384
01:02:12,933 --> 01:02:14,500
He was possessed
by a demon.

1385
01:02:14,533 --> 01:02:16,067
Now, dear, you know
there’s no such thing

1386
01:02:16,100 --> 01:02:18,000
as being possessed
by a demon.

1387
01:02:18,033 --> 01:02:19,233
That’s an old superstition.

1388
01:02:19,267 --> 01:02:20,933
Well, I don’t care
what you call it, dad.

1389
01:02:20,967 --> 01:02:22,133
But there’s something here
that’s evil.

1390
01:02:22,167 --> 01:02:23,667
It’s real,
and I can feel it.

1391
01:02:23,700 --> 01:02:24,933
And i’m afraid!

1392
01:02:24,967 --> 01:02:25,933
It makes me want to sing.

1393
01:02:25,967 --> 01:02:27,533
... any longer.
Let’s get away.

1394
01:02:27,567 --> 01:02:31,233
I can’t go now, dear, I must
wait till my work’s finished.

1395
01:02:31,267 --> 01:02:33,567
Pull yourself together,
dear.

1396
01:02:33,600 --> 01:02:36,267
Oh... I won’t
be silly anymore.

1397
01:02:36,300 --> 01:02:38,233
Nothing must interfere
with your work.

1398
01:02:38,267 --> 01:02:40,600
You’ve been
a very great help to me.

1399
01:02:40,633 --> 01:02:44,600
I know what a terribly lonely
life this must be for you.

1400
01:02:44,633 --> 01:02:46,967
We won’t talk about it
anymore, dad.

1401
01:02:47,000 --> 01:02:48,200
I just want you to prove

1402
01:02:48,233 --> 01:02:50,200
that you’re the greatest
scientist in the world.

1403
01:02:50,233 --> 01:02:53,033
They’ll soon have
all the proof they want.

1404
01:02:53,067 --> 01:02:55,600
I expect we plan on
professor Fitzgerald

1405
01:02:55,633 --> 01:02:57,733
to pay me a call
before long.

1406
01:02:57,767 --> 01:03:00,100
And I shall be
delighted to confound him

1407
01:03:00,133 --> 01:03:04,767
with scientific facts that
he declared were impossible!

1408
01:03:04,800 --> 01:03:06,600
(Chuckles)

1409
01:03:06,633 --> 01:03:08,333
Now, you go to bed,
dear, huh?

1410
01:03:08,367 --> 01:03:09,667
Have a good night’s rest?

1411
01:03:09,700 --> 01:03:11,233
Good night, dear.

1412
01:03:11,267 --> 01:03:13,067
Right, i’ll get
some warm milk.

1413
01:03:13,100 --> 01:03:14,067
Good night, dad.

1414
01:03:14,100 --> 01:03:15,567
What a kook.

1415
01:03:15,600 --> 01:03:16,900
Nice caboose
on that girl.

1416
01:03:16,933 --> 01:03:19,600
What am I saying--
that’s my daughter!

1417
01:03:19,633 --> 01:03:21,567
I am mad.

1418
01:03:32,733 --> 01:03:35,600
(Snarling)

1419
01:03:38,567 --> 01:03:41,100
Poor Petro wants to be
out with his friends,

1420
01:03:41,133 --> 01:03:44,600
people of his own kind,
playing and killing.

1421
01:03:44,633 --> 01:03:46,200
That’s the life.

1422
01:03:54,700 --> 01:03:56,533
Boy, they’re getting a lot
of use out of that swamp set,

1423
01:03:56,567 --> 01:03:57,800
aren’t they?

1424
01:03:57,833 --> 01:03:59,567
Yeah, but it’s not fog
hanging in the air,

1425
01:03:59,600 --> 01:04:01,000
it’s body odor.

1426
01:04:06,633 --> 01:04:09,967
It’s kind of like an early
music video, isn’t it?

1427
01:04:10,000 --> 01:04:13,567
Yeah, before
they invented action.

1428
01:04:15,600 --> 01:04:17,033
Or Paula Abdul.

1429
01:04:18,367 --> 01:04:20,033
[Knocking on door]

1430
01:04:22,733 --> 01:04:25,733
Look at that lantern.

1431
01:04:25,767 --> 01:04:28,600
That ladder casts a shadow
in the wrong direction.

1432
01:04:33,200 --> 01:04:35,633
Hi... sorry to bother you
this time of night,

1433
01:04:35,667 --> 01:04:36,900
but i’ve lost my way.

1434
01:04:36,933 --> 01:04:38,000
Come on in.

1435
01:04:38,033 --> 01:04:39,067
Water’s fine.

1436
01:04:40,733 --> 01:04:42,067
- Sit down.
- Sit a spell.

1437
01:04:42,100 --> 01:04:44,200
Take your shoes off.

1438
01:04:44,233 --> 01:04:46,100
You’re a fool to be
traveling around

1439
01:04:46,133 --> 01:04:47,633
by yourself
at night like this,

1440
01:04:47,667 --> 01:04:49,633
when the mist comes up
from the swampland.

1441
01:04:49,667 --> 01:04:51,700
Oh, you mean
the mysterious night prowler

1442
01:04:51,733 --> 01:04:53,900
that’s got everyone’s
imagination working overtime?

1443
01:04:53,933 --> 01:04:56,333
It ain’t imagination
with me, mister.

1444
01:04:56,367 --> 01:04:57,600
It’s a way of life.

1445
01:04:57,633 --> 01:04:59,900
That thing killed
my little girl.

1446
01:04:59,933 --> 01:05:01,133
I’m sorry, i--

1447
01:05:01,167 --> 01:05:02,733
I wouldn’t have joked
if i’d known--

1448
01:05:02,767 --> 01:05:05,867
scared jed Harper so bad,
he went and got religion.

1449
01:05:05,900 --> 01:05:07,200
But I hear there’s
a cure for that.

1450
01:05:07,233 --> 01:05:09,633
Can you give me
some facts about this killer?

1451
01:05:09,667 --> 01:05:10,700
What does it look like?

1452
01:05:10,733 --> 01:05:13,067
No one can tell you that
for sure, mister.

1453
01:05:13,100 --> 01:05:15,633
’cause it never prowls--
only on nights like this

1454
01:05:15,667 --> 01:05:16,833
when the mist is thick.

1455
01:05:16,867 --> 01:05:19,200
And nothing can be
seen very plain.

1456
01:05:19,233 --> 01:05:22,633
Jed Harper said
it traveled on two legs.

1457
01:05:22,667 --> 01:05:24,733
But you can’t count on that,
’cause jed was scared

1458
01:05:24,767 --> 01:05:25,933
half out of his mind.

1459
01:05:25,967 --> 01:05:27,100
And he’s an idiot.

1460
01:05:27,133 --> 01:05:30,667
Besides, the mist was
extra-thick that night.

1461
01:05:30,700 --> 01:05:33,600
You better stay here
the rest of the night, mister.

1462
01:05:33,633 --> 01:05:34,867
I can’t offer you a bed,

1463
01:05:34,900 --> 01:05:37,100
but i’ll make you
as comfortable as I can.

1464
01:05:37,133 --> 01:05:39,067
Well, thank you
very much.

1465
01:05:39,100 --> 01:05:40,933
You can sleep on me.

1466
01:05:40,967 --> 01:05:43,900
Oh, are you still here?

1467
01:05:46,767 --> 01:05:48,767
He’s looking for
his favorite tree.

1468
01:05:51,933 --> 01:05:53,667
Stick around,
there’s plenty more film.

1469
01:05:56,967 --> 01:06:00,667
That’s the most casually-dressed
monster i’ve ever seen.

1470
01:06:00,700 --> 01:06:04,533
Not really prowling,
he’s just kind of wandering.

1471
01:06:06,700 --> 01:06:08,600
Not this again.

1472
01:06:14,700 --> 01:06:17,633
Looks like, uh...

1473
01:06:17,667 --> 01:06:19,100
I don’t know if you
can tell in that shot,

1474
01:06:19,133 --> 01:06:21,767
but I think petro’s doing
something new with his hair.

1475
01:06:21,800 --> 01:06:23,533
Maybe some new mousse.

1476
01:06:23,567 --> 01:06:25,033
Maybe it’s a deer.

1477
01:06:28,733 --> 01:06:30,267
If I have to
keep you in a cage,

1478
01:06:30,300 --> 01:06:32,800
you’re of no further
use to me.

1479
01:06:32,833 --> 01:06:34,267
You’ll revert
without my knowledge

1480
01:06:34,300 --> 01:06:36,667
and become dangerous.

1481
01:06:36,700 --> 01:06:40,167
I hadn’t thought
of that possibility.

1482
01:06:40,200 --> 01:06:41,267
On your couch,
Petro!

1483
01:06:41,300 --> 01:06:42,733
(Snarling)

1484
01:06:42,767 --> 01:06:43,800
Now!

1485
01:06:43,833 --> 01:06:45,833
Couch...
Couch...

1486
01:06:45,867 --> 01:06:46,967
Couch, Petro, couch.

1487
01:06:47,000 --> 01:06:48,167
You know, there’s
gonna come a day

1488
01:06:48,200 --> 01:06:51,633
when he’s just
not gonna do that anymore.

1489
01:06:51,667 --> 01:06:53,967
Spare the whip
and spoil the wolf.

1490
01:06:54,000 --> 01:06:55,533
I don’t know,
Petro took, like,

1491
01:06:55,567 --> 01:06:56,933
a shotgun blast
to the chest,

1492
01:06:56,967 --> 01:06:58,467
but he’s afraid of a whip.

1493
01:06:58,500 --> 01:07:01,533
Something’s wrong.

1494
01:07:01,567 --> 01:07:04,533
He’ll never be a show dog
at this rate.

1495
01:07:04,567 --> 01:07:07,500
♪♪

1496
01:07:10,600 --> 01:07:13,633
That’s not
the normal syringe.

1497
01:07:13,667 --> 01:07:14,633
(Snarling)

1498
01:07:14,667 --> 01:07:15,633
Who?

1499
01:07:15,667 --> 01:07:18,833
Time for your
booster shot.

1500
01:07:18,867 --> 01:07:22,967
I couldn’t disappoint
professor Fitzgerald.

1501
01:07:23,000 --> 01:07:24,633
♪♪

1502
01:07:24,667 --> 01:07:27,567
Not a very decisive
mad scientist, is he?

1503
01:07:33,600 --> 01:07:35,633
Down, Petro!
Down!

1504
01:07:37,667 --> 01:07:40,100
That is the right way
to administer a shot.

1505
01:07:40,133 --> 01:07:41,500
Just tie off
the arm, find the vein,

1506
01:07:41,533 --> 01:07:42,600
knee on his chest,
and make sure

1507
01:07:42,633 --> 01:07:44,900
to line up
your shin with his sternum.

1508
01:07:57,067 --> 01:07:58,800
Line.

1509
01:07:58,833 --> 01:08:02,233
Did I walk
in my sleep again?

1510
01:08:02,267 --> 01:08:03,800
Yes.

1511
01:08:06,700 --> 01:08:09,867
Doctor, ain’t there some way
you could cure me

1512
01:08:09,900 --> 01:08:11,633
from doing that?

1513
01:08:11,667 --> 01:08:13,600
I’m afraid not.

1514
01:08:13,633 --> 01:08:16,000
It may be necessary
to lock you up at night

1515
01:08:16,033 --> 01:08:17,767
after this.

1516
01:08:17,800 --> 01:08:21,133
That don’t seem fair
to lock a man up,

1517
01:08:21,167 --> 01:08:25,133
just like he was an animal.

1518
01:08:25,167 --> 01:08:27,567
Neutering often helps
ease a bad temper.

1519
01:08:30,867 --> 01:08:33,833
You could stand petro’s overalls
in a corner.

1520
01:08:33,867 --> 01:08:35,933
... home with a jug
under his arm last night.

1521
01:08:35,967 --> 01:08:39,133
That’s a really bad toupee.

1522
01:08:39,167 --> 01:08:41,100
He’ll never lift
another jug.

1523
01:08:41,133 --> 01:08:43,400
We have this scene on
a commemorative plate, you know.

1524
01:08:43,433 --> 01:08:44,767
Yeah.

1525
01:08:44,800 --> 01:08:46,000
And he wasn’t a pretty sight
to look at, neither.

1526
01:08:46,033 --> 01:08:48,267
There must be some natural
explanation

1527
01:08:48,300 --> 01:08:50,533
for this thing
you’re talking about.

1528
01:08:50,567 --> 01:08:51,967
It’s a werewolf.

1529
01:08:52,000 --> 01:08:54,900
You can’t get him no way,
except by a silver bullet.

1530
01:08:54,933 --> 01:08:56,267
[All]
Shut up!

1531
01:08:56,300 --> 01:08:57,667
I’m not giving that story
to the city editor.

1532
01:08:57,700 --> 01:09:01,100
He’d throw me
right out on my ear.

1533
01:09:01,133 --> 01:09:02,767
That killer ain’t human.

1534
01:09:02,800 --> 01:09:06,567
You’d know that
from one look at the body.

1535
01:09:06,600 --> 01:09:09,967
I saw a victim of just
such an attack.

1536
01:09:10,000 --> 01:09:11,900
I wonder if there
could be a possibility

1537
01:09:11,933 --> 01:09:13,033
of any connection
between them.

1538
01:09:13,067 --> 01:09:14,100
[All]
Nah!

1539
01:09:14,133 --> 01:09:15,733
I’ve been telling you
all along,

1540
01:09:15,767 --> 01:09:18,733
it’s the form of a human
who sold his soul to the devil.

1541
01:09:18,767 --> 01:09:20,767
- That’s Tom waits’ mom.
- In the light of day,

1542
01:09:20,800 --> 01:09:23,633
he walks like
any other man on the earth.

1543
01:09:23,667 --> 01:09:26,733
At night, he takes on
the form of a wolf!

1544
01:09:26,767 --> 01:09:29,200
[All]
Shut up!

1545
01:09:29,233 --> 01:09:30,633
Then you never heard
of any killings...

1546
01:09:30,667 --> 01:09:32,700
- Ooh...
- By strange animals around here

1547
01:09:32,733 --> 01:09:35,100
until recently?

1548
01:09:35,133 --> 01:09:38,067
Why does he keep
asking me all them questions?

1549
01:09:38,100 --> 01:09:39,600
Well, all newspapermen
are like that.

1550
01:09:39,633 --> 01:09:41,000
He doesn’t mean
anything by it.

1551
01:09:41,033 --> 01:09:42,333
He’s just a jerk.

1552
01:09:42,367 --> 01:09:44,300
You go ahead--
he won’t bother you anymore.

1553
01:09:44,333 --> 01:09:46,267
Yes, miss.

1554
01:09:55,067 --> 01:09:56,700
Lenora, you must
give some thought

1555
01:09:56,733 --> 01:09:58,633
to your own safety
and peace of mind.

1556
01:09:58,667 --> 01:10:01,167
There’s no normal reason
for Dr. Cameron to hide away

1557
01:10:01,200 --> 01:10:02,667
in a place like this,
unless--

1558
01:10:02,700 --> 01:10:04,500
unless what?

1559
01:10:04,533 --> 01:10:05,733
Oh, nothing.
Forget it.

1560
01:10:05,767 --> 01:10:06,733
Don’t avoid the question.

1561
01:10:06,767 --> 01:10:08,100
What were you
going to say?

1562
01:10:08,133 --> 01:10:10,533
I was gonna say, "unless he’s
hiding something," okay?

1563
01:10:10,567 --> 01:10:11,967
Get off my back.

1564
01:10:12,000 --> 01:10:13,167
That creature is responsible

1565
01:10:13,200 --> 01:10:14,667
for the horrible killings
in this vicinity,

1566
01:10:14,700 --> 01:10:16,600
and I think the same creature
killed professor Blaine.

1567
01:10:16,633 --> 01:10:18,033
Well, that’s a terrible thing
for you to say.

1568
01:10:18,067 --> 01:10:19,967
I didn’t expect you to join
the chorus against him.

1569
01:10:20,000 --> 01:10:21,667
I had to join the chorus--
i’m a great tenor.

1570
01:10:21,700 --> 01:10:23,667
I hope i’m wrong,
but i’ll do everything I can

1571
01:10:23,700 --> 01:10:25,633
to learn the truth,
no matter where it leads.

1572
01:10:28,300 --> 01:10:31,200
Good morning, young man.
I’m very glad to see you.

1573
01:10:31,233 --> 01:10:33,867
I want to apologize for the way
I talked to you last night.

1574
01:10:33,900 --> 01:10:35,733
Oh, well,
that’s all right.

1575
01:10:35,767 --> 01:10:37,167
I-- I can understand
how you must feel

1576
01:10:37,200 --> 01:10:38,600
about the newspapers.

1577
01:10:38,633 --> 01:10:41,600
It isn’t what you call
"hang on with their punches"?

1578
01:10:41,633 --> 01:10:42,633
"Pull their punches."

1579
01:10:42,667 --> 01:10:44,600
I know-- they gave you
quite a ride.

1580
01:10:44,633 --> 01:10:46,600
It was the way
he was treated in "doonesbury"

1581
01:10:46,633 --> 01:10:47,967
that got him really mad.

1582
01:10:48,000 --> 01:10:50,633
I’m so sorry,
the way I received you.

1583
01:10:50,667 --> 01:10:53,067
If you’re interested
in scientific research,

1584
01:10:53,100 --> 01:10:55,733
i’d be glad sometime to show you
what i’ve accomplished.

1585
01:10:55,767 --> 01:10:58,233
Well, thanks, doc,
that’s very nice.

1586
01:10:58,267 --> 01:11:00,100
Now, if you’ll pardon me,

1587
01:11:00,133 --> 01:11:02,767
i’ll leave lenora
to entertain you.

1588
01:11:02,800 --> 01:11:03,833
Well, okay.

1589
01:11:03,867 --> 01:11:05,367
I gotta go.

1590
01:11:05,400 --> 01:11:08,167
That doesn’t sound like there’s
a secret to hide, does it?

1591
01:11:08,200 --> 01:11:09,333
Why, no.

1592
01:11:09,367 --> 01:11:10,867
I’m getting really
antsy watching this.

1593
01:11:10,900 --> 01:11:12,733
I never before was
so glad to be wrong.

1594
01:11:12,767 --> 01:11:19,800
[Mechanical whooshing,
whirring, and clanking]

1595
01:11:28,033 --> 01:11:30,300
Is this funny, Joel?
Is this supposed to be funny?

1596
01:11:30,333 --> 01:11:32,133
You’ll notice i’m not
laughing, Joel.

1597
01:11:32,167 --> 01:11:33,800
Hey, take it easy,
Tom Servo.

1598
01:11:33,833 --> 01:11:37,067
It’s just an experiment in
transmogrificational aconthropy,

1599
01:11:37,100 --> 01:11:39,033
just like in the film
we’re watching, you know?

1600
01:11:39,067 --> 01:11:41,633
A human being with
the features of a wolf.

1601
01:11:41,667 --> 01:11:42,667
That’s what’s going on here.

1602
01:11:42,700 --> 01:11:43,800
Oh, so it means
i’m supposed to go

1603
01:11:43,833 --> 01:11:45,800
roaming around the countryside,
ravaging people?

1604
01:11:45,833 --> 01:11:47,733
- No.
- I look like a geek, Joel.

1605
01:11:47,767 --> 01:11:49,167
No, don’t worry about it.

1606
01:11:49,200 --> 01:11:52,600
Just think of it like this--
you’re my Servo-Croatian.

1607
01:11:52,633 --> 01:11:54,267
- Yeah, that’s it.
- Servo-Croatian?

1608
01:11:54,300 --> 01:11:57,133
Very funny-- you turned us
into mutants for a pun, Joel.

1609
01:11:57,167 --> 01:11:58,867
Not funny.
Real mature.

1610
01:11:58,900 --> 01:12:00,933
Wow, what a party!

1611
01:12:00,967 --> 01:12:03,033
Wow, I had this
weirdest dream, guys.

1612
01:12:03,067 --> 01:12:06,300
I-- I dreamt I was ray milland
on rosey greer’s body.

1613
01:12:06,333 --> 01:12:08,700
[Laughing]
Hey-- hey, uh, Servo.

1614
01:12:08,733 --> 01:12:10,833
You look great.
That upper body is really cu--

1615
01:12:10,867 --> 01:12:12,900
hey, wait a minute, pal,
that’s my body!

1616
01:12:12,933 --> 01:12:14,767
Hey, when I said you could
borrow some of my stuff,

1617
01:12:14,800 --> 01:12:16,600
I didn’t mean my torso!
- Take it easy.

1618
01:12:16,633 --> 01:12:18,033
- Listen, crow,
here’s the story.

1619
01:12:18,067 --> 01:12:20,233
Dr. Joel f. Frankenstein
here

1620
01:12:20,267 --> 01:12:23,367
thought it’d be very, very funny
to switch our heads around.

1621
01:12:23,400 --> 01:12:27,633
Oh, great-- so you look like an
erector set with a goiter,

1622
01:12:27,667 --> 01:12:29,767
and i’ve got a body
dogs can’t resist.

1623
01:12:29,800 --> 01:12:31,200
Oh, that’s real funny.

1624
01:12:31,233 --> 01:12:32,367
You know what else
is real funny?

1625
01:12:32,400 --> 01:12:33,900
I just filled
your load pan.

1626
01:12:33,933 --> 01:12:36,333
- Oh, yeah?
- Hey, come on, come on.

1627
01:12:36,367 --> 01:12:37,700
Take it easy--
it’s an experiment.

1628
01:12:37,733 --> 01:12:39,100
Just tell me how you feel.

1629
01:12:39,133 --> 01:12:41,233
Well, do you want to know
where my head’s at, Joel?

1630
01:12:41,267 --> 01:12:42,967
Or do you want
his gut reaction?

1631
01:12:43,000 --> 01:12:45,200
[Laughter]

1632
01:12:45,233 --> 01:12:46,733
- Hey!
- Hey!

1633
01:12:46,767 --> 01:12:49,300
[Both in unison] Are you
thinking what i’m thinking?

1634
01:12:49,333 --> 01:12:51,300
If you like long walks
in the rain...

1635
01:12:51,333 --> 01:12:52,633
Chinese food...

1636
01:12:52,667 --> 01:12:53,867
Mushing up
your ice cream...

1637
01:12:53,900 --> 01:12:57,067
- Oh, yes, we love us!
- Oh, yes, we love us!

1638
01:12:57,100 --> 01:13:00,667
[Both] Our collective brains
are more powerful than Joel.

1639
01:13:00,700 --> 01:13:03,633
There’s nothing that can
stand in our way.

1640
01:13:03,667 --> 01:13:06,167
We have seen the future,
and it is us.

1641
01:13:06,200 --> 01:13:08,633
Massage us into your scalp.

1642
01:13:08,667 --> 01:13:12,167
We are one, all one,
all powerful.

1643
01:13:12,200 --> 01:13:16,167
Yes, we are Servo-Croatian,
and we shall rule the wor--

1644
01:13:16,200 --> 01:13:17,567
[click]

1645
01:13:17,600 --> 01:13:20,733
My robots--
I think i’ll keep ’em...

1646
01:13:20,767 --> 01:13:21,967
Turned off.

1647
01:13:22,000 --> 01:13:25,933
♪♪

1648
01:13:32,633 --> 01:13:34,167
[Knocking]

1649
01:13:34,200 --> 01:13:36,733
See, it was easy to switch
your heads back.

1650
01:13:36,767 --> 01:13:37,767
Thanks, Joel.

1651
01:13:37,800 --> 01:13:39,133
I don’t want to talk
about it.

1652
01:13:39,167 --> 01:13:40,133
Uh-oh, these-- oh.

1653
01:13:40,167 --> 01:13:42,033
Into my seat--
i’m comfy now.

1654
01:13:42,067 --> 01:13:43,733
Oh, professor Fitzgerald!

1655
01:13:43,767 --> 01:13:46,133
How is the world did you
find your way down here?

1656
01:13:46,167 --> 01:13:47,300
Zelda drove me.

1657
01:13:47,333 --> 01:13:48,367
Your father wrote,
asking me to come.

1658
01:13:48,400 --> 01:13:49,733
- Is he here?
- Yes.

1659
01:13:49,767 --> 01:13:51,700
And he’ll be very glad to see
one of his old friends.

1660
01:13:51,733 --> 01:13:54,767
Ah, Fitzgerald, it is
an unexpected honor.

1661
01:13:54,800 --> 01:13:57,333
I hardly dared hope that
you’d accept my invitation.

1662
01:13:57,367 --> 01:13:59,633
Well, if you’ve made a
worthwhile scientific discovery,

1663
01:13:59,667 --> 01:14:01,667
I want to be the first
to congratulate you.

1664
01:14:01,700 --> 01:14:03,700
I shall be delighted to
give you that opportunity.

1665
01:14:03,733 --> 01:14:05,367
Well, the things that you two
have to talk over

1666
01:14:05,400 --> 01:14:07,500
are way over my head,
so i’ll leave you alone.

1667
01:14:07,533 --> 01:14:08,867
Oh, now, don’t run away.

1668
01:14:08,900 --> 01:14:11,767
I promise to limit myself
to words of two syllables.

1669
01:14:11,800 --> 01:14:13,133
That’ll take all the fun
out of it.

1670
01:14:13,167 --> 01:14:14,800
Stop patronizing me,
you dork.

1671
01:14:14,833 --> 01:14:16,833
- I’ll see you after a while.
- Okay.

1672
01:14:16,867 --> 01:14:17,733
Shall we go into the lab?

1673
01:14:17,767 --> 01:14:19,733
If you wish.

1674
01:14:21,333 --> 01:14:22,800
Oh, through the bookcase,
huh?

1675
01:14:22,833 --> 01:14:25,733
Wish I had me one of them
bookcase doors.

1676
01:14:25,767 --> 01:14:29,333
[Growling]

1677
01:14:29,367 --> 01:14:31,833
You’re very "secreetive"
about your work.

1678
01:14:31,867 --> 01:14:33,100
No, secretive.

1679
01:14:33,133 --> 01:14:34,767
I like to keep out
intruders.

1680
01:14:34,800 --> 01:14:36,900
You seem to be
excellently equipped.

1681
01:14:36,933 --> 01:14:38,233
Thank you-- I didn’t
think you could tell

1682
01:14:38,267 --> 01:14:39,333
through these trousers.

1683
01:14:39,367 --> 01:14:40,700
Sit down, won’t you,
professor?

1684
01:14:40,733 --> 01:14:41,900
Thanks.

1685
01:14:49,000 --> 01:14:52,600
I’ve discovered that certain
extremely volatile elements

1686
01:14:52,633 --> 01:14:53,900
in the blood,

1687
01:14:53,933 --> 01:14:56,733
little more than particles
of electrical energy,

1688
01:14:56,767 --> 01:15:00,033
are the source of all
physical growth and mentality.

1689
01:15:00,067 --> 01:15:03,233
By exciting the various
glands and brain cells...

1690
01:15:03,267 --> 01:15:04,800
Sounds painful.

1691
01:15:04,833 --> 01:15:08,100
I’ve learned how to extract
and concentrate these elements

1692
01:15:08,133 --> 01:15:10,967
from the blood
of various animals.

1693
01:15:11,000 --> 01:15:13,800
I can control evolution.

1694
01:15:13,833 --> 01:15:15,300
I’ve discovered
the source of life!

1695
01:15:15,333 --> 01:15:16,600
Deep dish pizza?

1696
01:15:16,633 --> 01:15:17,867
You’re crazy.

1697
01:15:17,900 --> 01:15:19,800
Oh, I wouldn’t say that
to him if I were you.

1698
01:15:19,833 --> 01:15:22,267
[Laughing]

1699
01:15:22,300 --> 01:15:24,800
That has a familiar ring.

1700
01:15:24,833 --> 01:15:29,400
You told the newspapers
I was crazy once, didn’t you?

1701
01:15:29,433 --> 01:15:30,833
I didn’t know that you
invited me here

1702
01:15:30,867 --> 01:15:33,167
to reopen an old controversy
that was very disagreeable...

1703
01:15:33,200 --> 01:15:34,767
- Is that Mel blanc?
- To all concerned.

1704
01:15:34,800 --> 01:15:38,667
I came with the hope that you’d
abandoned impossible theories

1705
01:15:38,700 --> 01:15:40,700
and accomplished something
worthy of consideration.

1706
01:15:40,733 --> 01:15:41,867
Nice nostrils.

1707
01:15:41,900 --> 01:15:43,700
And I suppose you feel
yourself fully competent

1708
01:15:43,733 --> 01:15:46,167
to judge
my accomplishments--

1709
01:15:46,200 --> 01:15:48,167
you and your hide-bound
associates,

1710
01:15:48,200 --> 01:15:50,133
Hatfield, Warwick,
and Blaine!

1711
01:15:50,167 --> 01:15:52,733
Associates, and they’re
very "secreetive."

1712
01:15:52,767 --> 01:15:55,100
Now, Blaine,

1713
01:15:55,133 --> 01:15:59,367
he no longer is passing
judgment on my sanity.

1714
01:16:01,067 --> 01:16:05,100
I believe you know something
about blaine’s death.

1715
01:16:05,133 --> 01:16:07,733
You were at his house.

1716
01:16:07,767 --> 01:16:09,633
You talked to him
on the telephone

1717
01:16:09,667 --> 01:16:11,800
before I came
to your house.

1718
01:16:11,833 --> 01:16:14,133
Did he say then
that he was in any danger

1719
01:16:14,167 --> 01:16:15,700
of losing his life?

1720
01:16:15,733 --> 01:16:18,667
No, nothing threatened him
at that time.

1721
01:16:18,700 --> 01:16:19,800
Oh, very good point.

1722
01:16:19,833 --> 01:16:22,133
And I was with you
when he was killed.

1723
01:16:22,167 --> 01:16:23,733
Okay, another good point.

1724
01:16:23,767 --> 01:16:26,700
You pride yourself on not
indulging in fantastic theories.

1725
01:16:26,733 --> 01:16:29,233
What proof have you that
I was in any way implicated

1726
01:16:29,267 --> 01:16:30,700
in blaine’s death?

1727
01:16:30,733 --> 01:16:33,767
I have no definite proof.

1728
01:16:33,800 --> 01:16:35,800
You accuse me of being crazy
for what I claim

1729
01:16:35,833 --> 01:16:38,167
to have accomplished
with this apparatus,

1730
01:16:38,200 --> 01:16:41,867
but I can give you overwhelming
proof of what I say.

1731
01:16:41,900 --> 01:16:44,300
I can inject into your veins
a substance

1732
01:16:44,333 --> 01:16:46,667
that will give you
the strength of ten men.

1733
01:16:46,700 --> 01:16:47,933
And their hair.

1734
01:16:47,967 --> 01:16:50,333
Or, following the line
of evolution,

1735
01:16:50,367 --> 01:16:53,100
how would you like a pair
of donkey’s ears?

1736
01:16:53,133 --> 01:16:56,733
Oh, nice quip.
You really tagged him there.

1737
01:16:56,767 --> 01:16:59,067
I certainly will not
be the subject

1738
01:16:59,100 --> 01:17:02,100
for any of your experiments,
and i’m afraid it’s useless

1739
01:17:02,133 --> 01:17:04,400
for us to continue this
discussion any further.

1740
01:17:04,433 --> 01:17:05,867
You’re not leaving,
are you?

1741
01:17:05,900 --> 01:17:07,633
I see no reason for staying.

1742
01:17:07,667 --> 01:17:09,700
Open that secret panel.

1743
01:17:09,733 --> 01:17:11,933
So you don’t care to stay
and be convinced

1744
01:17:11,967 --> 01:17:13,767
that you were wrong.

1745
01:17:13,800 --> 01:17:16,933
I don’t care to be ridiculed
by a charlatan.

1746
01:17:16,967 --> 01:17:18,133
Or you.

1747
01:17:18,167 --> 01:17:21,800
Ah, ridicule-- that isn’t
pleasant, is it?

1748
01:17:21,833 --> 01:17:23,167
I know from experience.

1749
01:17:23,200 --> 01:17:25,067
I had nothing
to do with that.

1750
01:17:25,100 --> 01:17:28,800
It wasn’t my fault if the public
treated your crazy theories

1751
01:17:28,833 --> 01:17:30,867
with the ridicule
they deserved.

1752
01:17:30,900 --> 01:17:33,433
Hey, don’t slam the bookcase
on the way out, pal.

1753
01:17:33,467 --> 01:17:35,467
Can’t you see he’s hurting?

1754
01:17:35,500 --> 01:17:37,667
- Fitzgerald!
- Well?

1755
01:17:39,500 --> 01:17:41,233
- Would you do me a favor?
- What is it?

1756
01:17:41,267 --> 01:17:42,433
Good time to ask for
a favor.

1757
01:17:42,467 --> 01:17:44,367
I have to send
my hired man into town.

1758
01:17:44,400 --> 01:17:46,633
Do you mind taking him
with you?

1759
01:17:46,667 --> 01:17:49,033
All right-- I can do that
without any trouble.

1760
01:17:49,067 --> 01:17:51,067
Oh, thank you so much.

1761
01:17:51,100 --> 01:17:52,700
If you’ll wait in the car,

1762
01:17:52,733 --> 01:17:55,600
i’ll send him out to you
in a few minutes.

1763
01:17:55,633 --> 01:17:58,633
Hope your car seats
are scotchgarded.

1764
01:18:00,633 --> 01:18:03,333
Make sure to leave
the window open a crack.

1765
01:18:06,700 --> 01:18:08,600
Now,
get in the car, Petro.

1766
01:18:08,633 --> 01:18:10,667
Don’t keep professor
Fitzgerald waiting.

1767
01:18:10,700 --> 01:18:14,767
I should have known this trip
would be a waste of time.

1768
01:18:16,300 --> 01:18:20,033
I still hope to give you proof
that you can’t ignore.

1769
01:18:20,067 --> 01:18:23,067
Remember me to Hatfield and
Warwick when you see them again.

1770
01:18:23,100 --> 01:18:25,033
[Engine starting]

1771
01:18:26,833 --> 01:18:28,800
Bye.
See you again.

1772
01:18:28,833 --> 01:18:30,200
[Snickering]

1773
01:18:32,200 --> 01:18:34,700
Why does he have to kill them
to prove his point?

1774
01:18:34,733 --> 01:18:36,800
Can’t he just show them a pie
chart or something?

1775
01:18:36,833 --> 01:18:39,067
Why did professor Fitzgerald
leave so soon?

1776
01:18:39,100 --> 01:18:41,300
He wasn’t impressed
by the possibilities

1777
01:18:41,333 --> 01:18:42,800
in my line of research.

1778
01:18:42,833 --> 01:18:44,067
Oh, well,
don’t be disappointed.

1779
01:18:44,100 --> 01:18:46,000
That’s no credit
to his intelligence.

1780
01:18:46,033 --> 01:18:47,667
You’re a great scientist,

1781
01:18:47,700 --> 01:18:49,200
and someday you’re going
to startle the world.

1782
01:18:49,233 --> 01:18:52,967
You’re right, dear-- i’ll
startle the world.

1783
01:18:53,000 --> 01:18:55,733
By making
an even worse film.

1784
01:18:57,367 --> 01:19:01,133
Oh, great,
now she’s locked out.

1785
01:19:02,767 --> 01:19:07,167
[Howling]

1786
01:19:07,200 --> 01:19:09,667
Mmm, peas and carrots,
peas and carrots, mmm!

1787
01:19:09,700 --> 01:19:12,233
Hey, everybody try to stay
in the shot this time.

1788
01:19:12,267 --> 01:19:14,167
[Howling]

1789
01:19:14,200 --> 01:19:16,833
Boy, they should make
the swamp set a lot bigger.

1790
01:19:16,867 --> 01:19:18,333
[Grumbling]

1791
01:19:18,367 --> 01:19:21,300
Whoa, slow down, pal.

1792
01:19:21,333 --> 01:19:24,633
Now, you guys, if he
turns into a werewolf,

1793
01:19:24,667 --> 01:19:26,567
is he gonna want to stick
his head out the window?

1794
01:19:26,600 --> 01:19:28,033
- Yep.
- Think so?

1795
01:19:28,067 --> 01:19:31,200
Any self-respecting
werewolf would.

1796
01:19:31,233 --> 01:19:33,633
Dogs can’t resist it.

1797
01:19:33,667 --> 01:19:35,933
♪♪

1798
01:19:35,967 --> 01:19:40,200
[Laughing]

1799
01:19:40,233 --> 01:19:42,667
What a funny serum.

1800
01:19:42,700 --> 01:19:44,000
[Chuckling]

1801
01:19:44,033 --> 01:19:45,767
[Laughing]

1802
01:19:45,800 --> 01:19:47,033
♪♪

1803
01:19:47,067 --> 01:19:48,233
That’s fun.

1804
01:19:48,267 --> 01:19:49,667
Mm-hmm.

1805
01:19:49,700 --> 01:19:52,167
♪♪

1806
01:19:52,200 --> 01:19:54,833
Keep your eye on
the windshield wiper, Petro.

1807
01:19:54,867 --> 01:19:56,200
It’s not even raining.

1808
01:19:56,233 --> 01:19:57,800
Why are the windshield wipers
going?

1809
01:19:57,833 --> 01:20:00,633
Your eyes
are getting heavy.

1810
01:20:00,667 --> 01:20:02,733
So is this plot.

1811
01:20:02,767 --> 01:20:08,800
♪♪

1812
01:20:08,833 --> 01:20:12,067
Oh, very subtle. Very, very
nice. Nice camerawork.

1813
01:20:12,100 --> 01:20:15,700
I thought the windshield wiper
would obscure our vision.

1814
01:20:15,733 --> 01:20:19,267
♪♪

1815
01:20:19,300 --> 01:20:22,967
Wow, it looks like
hoyt axton, you guys.

1816
01:20:23,000 --> 01:20:25,967
It does-- he’s gonna kill us
with "a cat name kalamazoo."

1817
01:20:26,000 --> 01:20:28,867
♪♪

1818
01:20:28,900 --> 01:20:32,133
Guess who’s history
in this scene?

1819
01:20:32,167 --> 01:20:34,033
- That guy?
- Bingo.

1820
01:20:34,067 --> 01:20:35,100
[Growling]

1821
01:20:35,133 --> 01:20:36,300
Hey, don’t you know

1822
01:20:36,333 --> 01:20:37,800
you’re not supposed to
talk to the driver--

1823
01:20:37,833 --> 01:20:40,367
let alone kill him
when he’s driving?

1824
01:20:40,400 --> 01:20:42,367
Hey, that sounded like
it came from over there.

1825
01:20:42,400 --> 01:20:43,767
The swamp set.

1826
01:20:43,800 --> 01:20:48,133
♪♪

1827
01:20:48,167 --> 01:20:49,767
This one’s a keeper.

1828
01:20:49,800 --> 01:20:51,000
Well, I guess
it’s all right.

1829
01:20:51,033 --> 01:20:53,133
Humans wear mink stoles.

1830
01:20:53,167 --> 01:20:55,333
Gonna mount him
over the fireplace.

1831
01:20:55,367 --> 01:20:57,867
♪♪

1832
01:20:57,900 --> 01:20:59,767
[Growling]

1833
01:20:59,800 --> 01:21:01,933
Look, he’s got
an all-day sucker.

1834
01:21:01,967 --> 01:21:04,767
He’s mooning them.

1835
01:21:04,800 --> 01:21:07,433
I don’t think it counts if
you’re mooning someone else

1836
01:21:07,467 --> 01:21:09,867
or using their moon
to do it.

1837
01:21:09,900 --> 01:21:13,200
♪♪

1838
01:21:13,233 --> 01:21:17,067
Helicopter spin--
do a helicopter spin.

1839
01:21:17,100 --> 01:21:19,900
That’s an illegal hold.

1840
01:21:19,933 --> 01:21:21,700
- Look!
- [Gasping]

1841
01:21:21,733 --> 01:21:23,967
Don’t shoot--
you’ll hit the dead guy.

1842
01:21:24,000 --> 01:21:27,167
♪♪

1843
01:21:35,767 --> 01:21:39,400
[Growling]

1844
01:21:39,433 --> 01:21:40,867
That hurts.

1845
01:21:40,900 --> 01:21:42,700
[Gunfire]

1846
01:21:42,733 --> 01:21:45,800
♪♪

1847
01:21:45,833 --> 01:21:47,067
What the--

1848
01:21:47,100 --> 01:21:51,233
[mumbling]

1849
01:21:51,267 --> 01:21:54,067
♪♪

1850
01:21:54,100 --> 01:21:55,867
Professor Fitzgerald!

1851
01:21:55,900 --> 01:21:57,033
Is he dead?

1852
01:21:57,067 --> 01:21:58,733
He’s still alive
but unconscious.

1853
01:21:58,767 --> 01:21:59,800
Where can we take him?

1854
01:21:59,833 --> 01:22:01,333
We can take him
over to my place.

1855
01:22:01,367 --> 01:22:03,067
The old danfield house
is handier.

1856
01:22:03,100 --> 01:22:04,367
We’ll take him there.

1857
01:22:04,400 --> 01:22:05,933
I don’t know how badly
he’s hurt.

1858
01:22:05,967 --> 01:22:07,333
One of you men
go for a doctor.

1859
01:22:07,367 --> 01:22:10,533
- Yeah, move his spine a lot.
- Hey, take it easy.

1860
01:22:10,567 --> 01:22:14,500
♪♪

1861
01:22:18,200 --> 01:22:19,833
Of course, it’s a problem.

1862
01:22:19,867 --> 01:22:21,100
You turn your gardener
into a beast,

1863
01:22:21,133 --> 01:22:22,900
and the garden
goes to pot.

1864
01:22:22,933 --> 01:22:24,200
Dad, please come
to dinner.

1865
01:22:24,233 --> 01:22:25,733
You know I don’t
like to eat alone.

1866
01:22:25,767 --> 01:22:27,767
Oh, i’m not very hungry.
You go ahead and go, dear.

1867
01:22:27,800 --> 01:22:29,200
Are you feeling discouraged

1868
01:22:29,233 --> 01:22:32,400
because professor Fitzgerald
was unsympathetic?

1869
01:22:32,433 --> 01:22:35,767
No, he’s discouraged ’cause you
can’t read your line, babe.

1870
01:22:35,800 --> 01:22:38,667
[Overlapping chatter]

1871
01:22:38,700 --> 01:22:39,933
[Knocking]
Here it is.

1872
01:22:39,967 --> 01:22:41,900
I’ll answer it.

1873
01:22:45,333 --> 01:22:47,233
[Knocking]

1874
01:22:47,267 --> 01:22:48,800
Hey, dad,
the carolers are here!

1875
01:22:48,833 --> 01:22:49,933
What happened?

1876
01:22:49,967 --> 01:22:51,700
I found professor Fitzgerald
badly hurt.

1877
01:22:51,733 --> 01:22:53,900
It’s a charivari.

1878
01:22:53,933 --> 01:22:56,233
It’s a singing dead guy
telegram.

1879
01:22:56,267 --> 01:22:58,267
Oh, I didn’t make any nog.

1880
01:22:58,300 --> 01:23:00,800
He’s another victim of that
mysterious werewolf,

1881
01:23:00,833 --> 01:23:02,833
or whatever it is.

1882
01:23:07,067 --> 01:23:11,767
Now i’m gonna have to kill him
all over again.

1883
01:23:13,900 --> 01:23:17,867
Well, he should be paralyzed
everywhere by now.

1884
01:23:17,900 --> 01:23:20,300
Bounce him down
and up a lot.

1885
01:23:29,433 --> 01:23:30,900
Easy, now.

1886
01:23:30,933 --> 01:23:32,333
Hmm.

1887
01:23:32,367 --> 01:23:35,800
Hey, can I have
this guy’s suit?

1888
01:23:35,833 --> 01:23:38,167
The dead guy’s glowing.

1889
01:23:38,200 --> 01:23:39,867
One of the men
went after a doctor.

1890
01:23:39,900 --> 01:23:41,233
He should be here
before long.

1891
01:23:41,267 --> 01:23:42,900
There’s nothing more
we can do here,

1892
01:23:42,933 --> 01:23:45,100
so we’ll keep hunting
for that killer.

1893
01:23:45,133 --> 01:23:46,933
You don’t need to come
down with us, ma’am.

1894
01:23:46,967 --> 01:23:48,833
We can find our way out.

1895
01:23:49,900 --> 01:23:51,767
Good evening, sir.

1896
01:23:51,800 --> 01:23:53,767
That devil
got another victim.

1897
01:23:53,800 --> 01:23:55,767
Yes, so I see.

1898
01:23:55,800 --> 01:23:57,167
Nice job.

1899
01:23:57,200 --> 01:23:58,733
They’re all in shock.

1900
01:23:58,767 --> 01:24:00,933
They’ve never been
in such a bad film.

1901
01:24:00,967 --> 01:24:03,667
I was surprised to see
professor Fitzgerald

1902
01:24:03,700 --> 01:24:04,767
in this neighborhood.

1903
01:24:04,800 --> 01:24:07,100
He came to see me.

1904
01:24:07,133 --> 01:24:09,367
- How badly is he hurt?
- I don’t know yet.

1905
01:24:09,400 --> 01:24:10,967
We’ll find out as soon
as the doctor gets here.

1906
01:24:11,000 --> 01:24:12,867
We’ll know as soon
as we find his head.

1907
01:24:12,900 --> 01:24:14,833
If he regains consciousness,
we’ll find out

1908
01:24:14,867 --> 01:24:18,433
who or what has committed
these horrible killings.

1909
01:24:18,467 --> 01:24:20,267
Yes.

1910
01:24:20,300 --> 01:24:23,267
It’ll be very interesting to
hear what he has to say.

1911
01:24:23,300 --> 01:24:24,667
Yeah, real interesting.

1912
01:24:24,700 --> 01:24:26,867
[Howling]

1913
01:24:26,900 --> 01:24:29,833
Hey, here’s that dog eating
peanut butter again.

1914
01:24:31,867 --> 01:24:35,300
Hmm, wait, I just lost
my bearings.

1915
01:24:35,333 --> 01:24:38,833
Okay, here’s the end
of the swamp set,

1916
01:24:38,867 --> 01:24:41,433
and over there
is the mansion.

1917
01:24:41,467 --> 01:24:43,067
There’s the director.

1918
01:24:43,100 --> 01:24:44,833
Hmm, no.

1919
01:24:44,867 --> 01:24:47,333
Well, hmm.

1920
01:24:47,367 --> 01:24:48,700
The door’s ajar.

1921
01:24:48,733 --> 01:24:49,900
No, it’s a door.

1922
01:24:49,933 --> 01:24:51,933
[Laughing]
I love that.

1923
01:24:53,133 --> 01:24:55,467
Since this awful thing
happened to him,

1924
01:24:55,500 --> 01:24:58,700
I can’t feel any resentment
over the way he treated you.

1925
01:24:58,733 --> 01:25:00,167
Did you quarrel with him?

1926
01:25:00,200 --> 01:25:02,167
Yes, we quarreled.

1927
01:25:02,200 --> 01:25:04,300
So I suppose you think
that I went after him

1928
01:25:04,333 --> 01:25:05,700
and dragged him
out of the car.

1929
01:25:05,733 --> 01:25:06,700
Dad!

1930
01:25:06,733 --> 01:25:07,800
His newspaper training

1931
01:25:07,833 --> 01:25:10,700
seems to have made
a suspicious nature.

1932
01:25:10,733 --> 01:25:13,800
So defensive!

1933
01:25:13,833 --> 01:25:15,833
This movie really
kicks into high gear, I bet,

1934
01:25:15,867 --> 01:25:16,867
right about now.

1935
01:25:16,900 --> 01:25:18,967
Well, maybe not.

1936
01:25:19,000 --> 01:25:22,700
Hey, what’s wolfman doing
down in the wine cellar?

1937
01:25:22,733 --> 01:25:23,900
He’s trying to figure out

1938
01:25:23,933 --> 01:25:25,833
what kind of wine goes
with people, I think.

1939
01:25:25,867 --> 01:25:26,767
Oh.

1940
01:25:26,800 --> 01:25:28,300
[Thunder crashing]

1941
01:25:28,333 --> 01:25:30,100
I wish you wouldn’t try
to antagonize him.

1942
01:25:30,133 --> 01:25:31,733
I wasn’t trying
to antagonize him.

1943
01:25:31,767 --> 01:25:33,033
He didn’t give me
a chance to finish.

1944
01:25:33,067 --> 01:25:35,467
I was just gonna ask him
if anyone was with Fitzgerald

1945
01:25:35,500 --> 01:25:36,733
when he left here.

1946
01:25:36,767 --> 01:25:37,833
Well, I can answer that.

1947
01:25:37,867 --> 01:25:38,833
Petro was with him.

1948
01:25:38,867 --> 01:25:39,800
Petro?

1949
01:25:39,833 --> 01:25:41,633
Yes, he drove him
into town.

1950
01:25:41,667 --> 01:25:42,867
Well, surely
you don’t think that--

1951
01:25:42,900 --> 01:25:44,033
I don’t know what to think.

1952
01:25:44,067 --> 01:25:45,167
And stop calling
me "Shirley."

1953
01:25:45,200 --> 01:25:47,767
But I will know as soon
as Fitzgerald comes to.

1954
01:25:47,800 --> 01:25:50,400
I’m inclined to agree with dad
about your imagination.

1955
01:25:50,433 --> 01:25:51,400
It’s fantastic.

1956
01:25:51,433 --> 01:25:52,400
Do you really like it?

1957
01:25:52,433 --> 01:25:53,700
But didn’t it
strike you funny

1958
01:25:53,733 --> 01:25:54,767
that we didn’t
find Petro around

1959
01:25:54,800 --> 01:25:56,400
when we found Fitzgerald?

1960
01:25:56,433 --> 01:25:58,433
Look, darling, please
don’t snap at me

1961
01:25:58,467 --> 01:26:00,767
just for trying
to add things up.

1962
01:26:00,800 --> 01:26:02,300
I’m sorry.

1963
01:26:02,333 --> 01:26:04,933
It’s arithmetic--
it always gets me.

1964
01:26:06,333 --> 01:26:10,833
Does the word "plodding"
mean anything to anyone?

1965
01:26:16,333 --> 01:26:17,933
He’s still unconscious.

1966
01:26:17,967 --> 01:26:19,800
Oh, he must regain
consciousness,

1967
01:26:19,833 --> 01:26:21,033
even if only
for a few minutes.

1968
01:26:21,067 --> 01:26:22,867
He’s throwing
a lot more light now.

1969
01:26:22,900 --> 01:26:24,267
That tie’s loud enough
to wake up anyone!

1970
01:26:24,300 --> 01:26:25,433
That must be the doctor.

1971
01:26:25,467 --> 01:26:27,800
Oh, i’ll go down with you.

1972
01:26:35,733 --> 01:26:36,933
Hey.

1973
01:26:36,967 --> 01:26:38,333
How’d he get upstairs?

1974
01:26:38,367 --> 01:26:41,267
Oh, I think he’s got an elevator
behind the fireplace.

1975
01:26:41,300 --> 01:26:43,267
Those mad scientists--
hoo!

1976
01:26:43,300 --> 01:26:44,400
They think of everything.

1977
01:26:44,433 --> 01:26:45,400
Oh, doctor, i’m glad
you got here.

1978
01:26:45,433 --> 01:26:46,800
I’m glad to get inside.

1979
01:26:46,833 --> 01:26:48,733
There’s a humdinger
of a storm coming up.

1980
01:26:48,767 --> 01:26:49,867
Never actually seen
a dinger hum.

1981
01:26:49,900 --> 01:26:50,967
Upstairs.

1982
01:26:51,000 --> 01:26:52,233
It’s raining cats and dogs.

1983
01:26:52,267 --> 01:26:54,033
You can go right on up.

1984
01:26:54,067 --> 01:26:57,733
You stay down here, dear.
There’s nothing you can do.

1985
